window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse12-acc-analysis","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Analysis of Financial Statements · Practice","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","subject":"Accountancy","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":1,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Sapna Ltd. was Rs. 8,00,000 in 2022-23 and Rs. 10,00,000 in 2023-24. In the comparative statement of profit and loss, what is the percentage change in revenue from operations?","image":"","options":["20% increase","25% increase","2% increase","12.5% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: First year = Rs. 8,00,000; Second year = Rs. 10,00,000.\nAbsolute change = 10,00,000 – 8,00,000 = Rs. 2,00,000 (increase).\nFormula: % change = Absolute change ÷ First year figure × 100.\nSubstituting: 2,00,000 ÷ 8,00,000 × 100 = 25%.\nThe 20% option comes from wrongly dividing by the second year figure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":2,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Kaveri Ltd. is preparing its Common Size Statement of Profit and Loss for the year ended March 31, 2024. It has the following information:\nRevenue from operations: Rs. 8,00,000\nOther income: Rs. 40,000\nEmployee benefit expenses: Rs. 2,00,000\nOther expenses: Rs. 1,20,000\nIncome tax: 30% of profit before tax\n\nWhat percentage will 'Profit after tax' show in the common size statement?","image":"","options":["45.50%","43.33%","65.00%","42.00%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations = Rs. 8,00,000. In a common size statement of profit and loss, revenue from operations is the base and is taken as 100.\nTotal revenue = 8,00,000 + 40,000 = Rs. 8,40,000. Profit before tax = 8,40,000 − (2,00,000 + 1,20,000) = Rs. 5,20,000.\nTax = 30% of 5,20,000 = Rs. 1,56,000. So profit after tax = Rs. 3,64,000.\nPAT % = 3,64,000 ÷ 8,00,000 × 100 = 45.50%.\nDividing by total revenue (Rs. 8,40,000) uses the wrong base and gives 43.33%. 65% is profit before tax, not profit after tax.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":3,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The net profit of Sunrise Ltd. fell from ₹12,00,000 in 2023-24 to ₹8,00,000 in 2024-25, even though its revenue from operations increased. The analyst studies the expense items of both years to find out exactly which expenses caused the fall in profit. Which objective of financial statement analysis does this work primarily serve?","image":"","options":["To ascertain the relative importance of different components of the financial position","To identify the reasons for change in the profitability of the firm","To judge the ability of the firm to repay its long-term debt","To assess the short-term liquidity position of the firm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The analyst is not just noting that profit fell. She is searching for why it fell despite higher revenue.\nThis matches the NCERT objective 'to identify the reasons for change in the profitability/financial position of the firm'.\nOption (1) is tempting, but relative importance concerns the share of each component in the total. It does not explain a change between years.\nOptions (3) and (4) relate to debt-repaying ability and liquidity, which this case does not involve.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":4,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Two companies in the same industry have the following Balance Sheet figures as at March 31, 2017:\nMohan Ltd.: Share capital Rs. 9,00,000; Reserves and surplus Rs. 3,00,000; Long-term borrowings Rs. 4,00,000; Trade payables Rs. 4,00,000; Total Rs. 20,00,000.\nSohan Ltd.: Share capital Rs. 15,00,000; Reserves and surplus Rs. 3,00,000; Long-term borrowings Rs. 9,00,000; Trade payables Rs. 3,00,000; Total Rs. 30,00,000.\nA common size balance sheet is prepared for an inter-firm comparison, taking total equity and liabilities as 100. By how many percentage points does the long-term borrowings share of Sohan Ltd. exceed that of Mohan Ltd.?","image":"","options":["10 percentage points","5 percentage points","30 percentage points","125 percentage points"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Long-term borrowings are Rs. 4,00,000 out of Rs. 20,00,000 (Mohan Ltd.) and Rs. 9,00,000 out of Rs. 30,00,000 (Sohan Ltd.).\nMohan Ltd.: $\\frac{4}{20} \\times 100 = 20\\%$.\nSohan Ltd.: $\\frac{9}{30} \\times 100 = 30\\%$.\nDifference = 30 − 20 = 10 percentage points.\nWhy not 125: that is the percentage rise in absolute amounts, $\\frac{9-4}{4} \\times 100$. This is horizontal (comparative) logic, not vertical analysis.\nWhy not 5: that is the absolute gap of Rs. 5 lakh.\nWhy not 30: that is Sohan Ltd.'s own share, not the difference.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":5,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In Year 1, Tara Ltd. had total assets of ₹50,00,000 and inventories of ₹5,00,000. In Year 2, inventories fell by 10% and total assets fell to ₹30,00,000.\nStatement I: Since the absolute amount of inventories fell, their percentage in the common size balance sheet must also fall in Year 2.\nStatement II: Inventories will be shown at 15% of total assets in the Year 2 common size balance sheet.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Year 1: inventories = 5,00,000 ÷ 50,00,000 × 100 = 10%.\nYear 2: inventories = 5,00,000 × 0.9 = ₹4,50,000.\nYear 2 percentage = 4,50,000 ÷ 30,00,000 × 100 = 15%.\nStatement I is incorrect. A common size percentage depends on the item relative to the base. Here total assets fell faster than inventories, so the percentage rose from 10% to 15%.\nStatement II is correct.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":6,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Analysis\n(B) Interpretation\n(C) Cross-sectional analysis\n(D) Time series analysis\nList-II\n(i) Comparison with the firm's own performance over a period of time\n(ii) Explaining the meaning and significance of data\n(iii) Simplification of data by methodical classification\n(iv) Comparison with other firms","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Analysis means simplifying data by methodical classification, so it matches (iii).\n(B) Interpretation means explaining the meaning and significance of data, so it matches (ii).\n(C) Cross-sectional analysis compares the firm with other firms, so it matches (iv).\n(D) Time series analysis compares the firm with its own performance over time, so it matches (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":7,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"From the following data of Vinay Ltd., find the percentage change in profit after tax shown in its comparative statement of profit and loss:\nRevenue from operations: 2022-23 Rs. 20,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 25,00,000\nExpenses: 2022-23 Rs. 12,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 14,00,000\nIncome tax rate: 40% in both years","image":"","options":["25% increase","37.5% increase","22.5% increase","50% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Profit before tax:\nYear 1 = 20,00,000 – 12,00,000 = Rs. 8,00,000\nYear 2 = 25,00,000 – 14,00,000 = Rs. 11,00,000\nProfit after tax at 40% tax:\nYear 1 = 8,00,000 × 60% = Rs. 4,80,000\nYear 2 = 11,00,000 × 60% = Rs. 6,60,000\nAbsolute change = Rs. 1,80,000.\n% change = 1,80,000 ÷ 4,80,000 × 100 = 37.5%. Since the tax rate is the same in both years, PBT also rises by 37.5%.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":8,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"An analyst of Sunrise Ltd. expresses every asset as a percentage of total assets, and every item of expenditure as a percentage of revenue from operations. This lets her compare Sunrise Ltd. with a much larger firm in the same industry. Which tool of financial analysis is she using?","image":"","options":["Comparative statements (horizontal analysis)","Common size statements (vertical analysis)","Trend analysis","Cash flow analysis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Common size statements express each item as a percentage of a common base. The base is total assets for the balance sheet and revenue from operations for the statement of profit and loss.\nThis is called vertical analysis. It brings firms of different sizes to a common base, so inter-firm comparison becomes possible.\nComparative statements place the absolute figures of two periods side by side and show the changes. They do not use a common base.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":9,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The common size income statement of Moti Ltd. shows cost of goods sold at 62.5% and operating expenses at 12.5% of revenue from operations. There are no other items above operating income. If operating income is Rs. 2,00,000, what is the revenue from operations?","image":"","options":["Rs. 8,00,000","Rs. 5,33,333","Rs. 16,00,000","Rs. 2,66,667"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: COGS = 62.5% and operating expenses = 12.5% of revenue.\nOperating income % = 100 − 62.5 − 12.5 = 25%\nSo 25% of revenue = Rs. 2,00,000.\nRevenue = 2,00,000 ÷ 0.25 = Rs. 8,00,000\nRs. 5,33,333 wrongly treats 37.5% (the gross profit percentage) as operating income.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":10,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the context of financial statement analysis, the term 'analysis' refers to:","image":"","options":["Explaining the meaning and significance of financial data","Simplification of financial data by methodical classification","Preparation of the balance sheet and statement of profit and loss","Verification of books of accounts by an auditor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says financial analysis covers both 'analysis' and 'interpretation'.\nAnalysis means simplifying financial data by methodically classifying the figures given in the financial statements.\nOption (1) describes interpretation, not analysis.\nPreparing statements and auditing come before analysis and are separate activities.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":11,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Figures in a comparative statement are truly comparable only when the same accounting principles are used to prepare the statements of both years.\nStatement II: If the accounting principles have changed between the two years, the deviation should be mentioned as a footnote to the comparative statement.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT says financial data will be comparative only when the same accounting principles are used in preparing these statements.\nStatement II is correct. Where different principles are used, the deviation should be mentioned as a footnote.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":12,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Neel Ltd. in the base year 2013-14 was ₹5,00,000. The trend percentage of revenue for 2015-16 was 90%. In 2016-17, revenue rose by 20% over 2015-16. What is the trend percentage of revenue for 2016-17?","image":"","options":["110%","108%","120%","130%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: base year revenue ₹5,00,000; trend % for 2015-16 = 90%.\nRevenue 2015-16 = 90% of 5,00,000 = ₹4,50,000.\nRevenue 2016-17 = 4,50,000 × 1.20 = ₹5,40,000.\nFormula: trend % = current year figure ÷ base year figure × 100.\nSo trend % = 5,40,000 ÷ 5,00,000 × 100 = 108%.\nSimply adding 20 to 90 gives 110%, which is wrong because the 20% rise is on the 2015-16 figure, not on the base.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":13,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In XRI Ltd., the share of non-current investments in total assets rose from 24.10% (2016) to 30.77% (2017), even though the amount stayed at Rs 10,00,000 in both years.\nStatement II: This rise in percentage happened because total assets fell from Rs 41,50,000 to Rs 32,50,000.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: 10,00,000 ÷ 41,50,000 = 24.10%, and 10,00,000 ÷ 32,50,000 = 30.77%.\nStatement II is correct: the numerator did not change, so the higher share comes only from the smaller base (total assets).\nLesson: a change in a common size percentage does not always mean the item's amount changed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":14,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"As at March 31, 2017, XRI Ltd. had non-current investments of Rs 10,00,000. Its total assets were Rs 32,50,000. What percentage will non-current investments show in the common size balance sheet for 2017?","image":"","options":["30.77%","24.10%","23.53%","33.33%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Non-current investments = Rs 10,00,000; Total assets = Rs 32,50,000.\nFormula: Item ÷ Total assets × 100.\nSubstituting: 10,00,000 ÷ 32,50,000 × 100 = 30.77.\nSo the share is 30.77%. The figure 24.10% is the 2016 share, when total assets were Rs 41,50,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":15,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Interpretation of financial statements without analysis is difficult or even impossible.\nReason (R): Analysis simplifies financial data by methodical classification, which provides the base on which its meaning and significance can be explained.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that analysis and interpretation are complementary. Analysis is useless without interpretation, and interpretation without analysis is difficult or even impossible.\nR is true: analysis simplifies data by methodical classification.\nR also explains A. Interpretation explains the meaning of data, so it needs the data to be simplified and classified first. Without analysis, there is nothing organised to interpret.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":16,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Shree Traders supplies raw material to a company on 30 days' credit. When it analyses the company's financial statements, which aspect will it focus on most?","image":"","options":["The company's capital structure and its ability to repay long-term loans","The company's liquidity, that is, its ability to meet short-term obligations","The company's future earnings that decide whether its shares should be held","The company's system of internal control and the performance of its managers"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Shree Traders is a trade payable (supplier on credit). Trade payables are interested in the firm's ability to meet their claims over a very short period.\nSo their analysis evaluates the firm's liquidity position.\nOption (a) is the concern of long-term lenders.\nOption (c) is the concern of investors.\nOption (d) is the concern of top management.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":17,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Aditya Ltd. (total assets Rs 10,00,000) and Anjali Ltd. (total assets Rs 12,00,000) can be meaningfully compared through common size balance sheets.\nReason (R): Common size statements convert every item into a percentage of a common total, which brings firms of different sizes to a common base.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: common size analysis makes inter-firm comparison possible even when the firms differ in size.\nR is true: each item is expressed as a percentage of total assets or total liabilities, taken as 100.\nIt is this common base that removes the effect of size, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":18,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about common size statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["They are useful for intra-firm comparison over different years as well as for inter-firm comparison.","In a common size balance sheet, each liability is shown as a percentage of total liabilities.","When prepared for successive periods, they show changes in the respective percentages over time.","Common size analysis is also known as horizontal analysis."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT says common size statements help in both intra-firm and inter-firm comparisons.\n(2) Correct: each liability is expressed as a percentage of total liabilities.\n(3) Correct: preparing them for successive years shows how the percentages change over time.\n(4) Incorrect: common size analysis is vertical analysis. Horizontal analysis refers to comparative statements.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":19,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"From the following data of Surya Ltd., calculate the percentage change in Profit after Tax that would appear in its comparative statement of profit and loss:\nRevenue from operations: 2015-16 ₹40,00,000; 2016-17 ₹50,00,000\nExpenses: 2015-16 ₹24,00,000; 2016-17 ₹28,00,000\nTax rate: 40% in both years","image":"","options":["25%","37.5%","27.27%","60%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue ₹40,00,000 and ₹50,00,000; expenses ₹24,00,000 and ₹28,00,000; tax 40% in both years.\nProfit before tax: 40,00,000 − 24,00,000 = ₹16,00,000 and 50,00,000 − 28,00,000 = ₹22,00,000.\nProfit after tax (60% of PBT): ₹9,60,000 and ₹13,20,000.\nAbsolute increase = ₹3,60,000.\nFormula: % change = absolute change ÷ first year figure × 100.\nSo % change = 3,60,000 ÷ 9,60,000 × 100 = 37.5%.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":20,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The balance sheet of Pranav Ltd. shows total assets of Rs 50,00,000. Goodwill appears at Rs 12,00,000. What percentage will goodwill show in the common size balance sheet?","image":"","options":["24%","30%","31.58%","12%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Goodwill = Rs 12,00,000; Total assets = Rs 50,00,000.\nFormula: Common size % = Item ÷ Total assets × 100.\nSubstituting: 12,00,000 ÷ 50,00,000 × 100 = 24.\nSo goodwill = 24% of total assets.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":21,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"While preparing a comparative balance sheet, the percentage increase or decrease in an item (column 5) is calculated by dividing the absolute change (column 4) by:","image":"","options":["the second-year figure of that item","the first-year figure of that item","the balance sheet total of the second year","the average of the two years' figures of that item"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Per NCERT, Step 3 of preparing comparative statements computes % change = Absolute increase or decrease (Col. 4) ÷ First year absolute figure (Col. 2) × 100.\nThe first year acts as the base for measuring change. Dividing by the second-year figure would understate increases.\nDividing by the balance sheet total gives a common size (vertical analysis) percentage, not a comparative one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":22,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Trend analysis uses the previous years' data of an enterprise to observe percentage changes over time in selected items.\nStatement II: In trend analysis, every item of a single year is expressed as a percentage of a common total such as revenue from operations or total assets.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. NCERT says trend analysis uses the previous years' data to observe percentage changes over time in selected data.\nStatement II is false. Expressing each item of a year as a percentage of a common total, such as revenue from operations or total assets, is common size (vertical) analysis.\nTrend analysis instead compares the same item across years with its base-year value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":23,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the common size income statement of Sona Ltd., revenue from operations is Rs. 20,00,000, cost of goods sold is Rs. 13,00,000 and operating expenses are Rs. 2,40,000. What is operating income as a percentage of revenue from operations?","image":"","options":["23%","35%","12%","77%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: revenue = Rs. 20,00,000, cost of goods sold = Rs. 13,00,000, operating expenses = Rs. 2,40,000. \nFormula: operating income = revenue − cost of goods sold − operating expenses. It is expressed as a % of revenue, which is the base of 100. \nSubstituting: operating income = 20,00,000 − 13,00,000 − 2,40,000 = Rs. 4,60,000. Then 4,60,000 ÷ 20,00,000 × 100 = 23. \nSo operating income is 23% of revenue. The figure 35% is the gross profit percentage. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":24,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following pairs of a user and the purpose of its financial analysis is INCORRECTLY matched?","image":"","options":["Trade payables: evaluate the firm's liquidity position, as they want their claims met over a very short period","Long-term lenders: study the relationship between various sources of funds (capital structure relationships)","Labour unions: judge whether a wage increase can be absorbed through increased productivity or by raising prices","Economists and researchers: evaluate the efficiency of management to decide whether a change in management is needed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct pair: trade payables focus on short-term liquidity.\n(2) Correct pair: lenders analyse capital structure relationships, along with interest and principal repayment ability.\n(3) Correct pair: labour unions assess whether a wage increase can be absorbed through productivity or prices.\n(4) Incorrect pair: evaluating management efficiency and deciding whether a change is needed is a purpose of investors. Economists and researchers study present business and economic conditions.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":25,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): While preparing a common size income statement, depreciation given separately should be added to operating expenses before operating income is computed.\nReason (R): Depreciation is a non-operating expense.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: In the NCERT illustration, depreciation is treated as part of operating expenses. So it is included before arriving at operating income (Gross profit − Operating expenses).\nR is false: Depreciation arises from using assets in normal operations. It is therefore an operating expense, not a non-operating one.\nNon-operating expenses are deducted only after operating income is found.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":26,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Common size statement\n(B) Comparative statement\n(C) Common base in common size statement of profit and loss\n(D) Common base in common size balance sheet\nList-II\n(i) Horizontal analysis\n(ii) Total assets or total liabilities\n(iii) Vertical analysis\n(iv) Revenue from operations","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Common size statement: this is vertical analysis, so (iii).\n(B) Comparative statement: this is horizontal analysis, so (i).\n(C) The base for the statement of profit and loss is revenue from operations, so (iv).\n(D) The base for the balance sheet is total assets or total liabilities, so (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":27,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of financial analysis as stated in NCERT?\n(i) It does not consider price level changes.\n(ii) It helps trade payables judge the liquidity of the firm.\n(iii) Only monetary information is considered.\n(iv) It may be misleading without knowledge of changes in accounting procedure.\n(v) It enables inter-firm comparison through common size statements.\n(vi) It does not reflect the current position because statements are based on accounting concepts.","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Limitation: price level changes are ignored.\n(ii) Not a limitation: this is a use of financial analysis to trade payables.\n(iii) Limitation: non-monetary aspects are ignored.\n(iv) Limitation: changes in accounting procedure can mislead.\n(v) Not a limitation: inter-firm comparison is an advantage of common size statements.\n(vi) Limitation: the figures do not reflect the current position.\nSo the limitations are (i), (iii), (iv) and (vi), which makes four.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":28,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: By analysing the financial statements of various firms, an economist can judge the extent of concentration of economic power and the pitfalls in the financial policies pursued.\nStatement II: The government relies on financial statement analysis only for taxation and not for actions such as licensing, fixing of prices or dividend freeze.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true: NCERT states that an economist can judge the concentration of economic power and the pitfalls in financial policies through such analysis.\nStatement II is false: NCERT says the analysis is the basis for many governmental actions. These include licensing, controls, fixing of prices, ceiling on profits, dividend freeze, and tax subsidy and other concessions to the corporate sector. Its use is not limited to taxation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":29,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about financial analysis is correct?","image":"","options":["Financial analysis reflects the current position because statements are prepared at market values","Financial analysis is completely free from personal judgement","Financial analysis is just a study of reports of the company","Financial analysis gives equal weight to monetary and non-monetary information"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Incorrect: Statements are prepared on accounting concepts such as historical cost, so they do not reflect the current position.\n(b) Incorrect: Personal judgement affects choices such as the depreciation method and provisions. NCERT names it as a limitation.\n(c) Correct: NCERT lists 'financial analysis is just a study of reports of the company' as a limitation.\n(d) Incorrect: Only monetary information is considered in financial analysis.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":30,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match the situations in List-I with the limitations of financial analysis in List-II:\nList-I\nA. A company switched its inventory valuation method from FIFO to weighted average and did not disclose it\nB. A building bought 15 years ago is shown at cost though its market value has tripled\nC. Just before the year-end, the company postponed purchases and pressed debtors for payment so that its cash balance looked strong\nD. Serious disputes between management and the labour union are not visible in the statements\nList-II\n1. Non-monetary aspects are ignored\n2. Window dressing\n3. Price level changes are not considered\n4. Misleading due to changes in accounting procedure","image":"","options":["A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1","A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3","A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-4: An undisclosed change in the inventory method distorts the comparison between years, so it is a change in accounting procedure.\nB-3: Showing the building at old cost ignores the rise in prices, so price level changes are not considered.\nC-2: Arranging transactions at year-end to show a better cash position is window dressing.\nD-1: Labour disputes cannot be expressed in money, so they fall under non-monetary aspects being ignored.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":31,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The comparative balance sheet of Neel Ltd. shows that its long-term borrowings increased by 40% during 2023-24. The long-term borrowings on March 31, 2024 stood at Rs. 7,00,000. What were the long-term borrowings on March 31, 2023?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,00,000","Rs. 4,20,000","Rs. 9,80,000","Rs. 2,80,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: 2024 figure = Rs. 7,00,000, and the increase is 40% of the 2023 figure.\nFormula: 2024 figure = 2023 figure × (1 + 0.40)\nSubstituting: 2023 figure = $\\frac{7,00,000}{1.40}$ = Rs. 5,00,000\nCheck: the increase is 2,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000 = 40%.\nThe option Rs. 4,20,000 wrongly deducts 40% of Rs. 7,00,000, ignoring that the percentage is based on the 2023 figure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":32,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Analysis of financial data is useless without interpretation.\nReason (R): Interpretation explains the meaning and significance of the data that has been simplified through analysis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says analysis is useless without interpretation, and interpretation without analysis is difficult or even impossible. The two are complementary.\nR is true. Interpretation means explaining the meaning and significance of the data.\nSimplified figures alone do not support any decision until their meaning is explained. That is exactly why analysis needs interpretation, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":33,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If a company changes its method of charging depreciation during the year, a comparative statement of profit and loss of the two years may give a misleading picture.\nReason (R): Financial data are truly comparative only when the same accounting principles are used in preparing the statements of both periods.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: The change in depreciation method alters the expense figures. The year-to-year change in profit then partly reflects the new method, not the actual performance.\nR is true: NCERT states that data are comparative only when the same accounting principles are used. Any deviation should be shown as a footnote.\nR explains A: The comparison becomes misleading precisely because the principles applied in the two years differ.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":34,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"From the following data of Rangoli Paints Ltd., calculate the percentage change in Profit after tax:\nRevenue from operations: 2022-23 Rs. 30,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 36,00,000\nOther income: Rs. 1,00,000 in both years\nCost of materials consumed: Rs. 12,00,000; Rs. 15,00,000\nChanges in inventories of finished goods: Rs. (50,000); Rs. 1,00,000\nEmployee benefit expenses: Rs. 4,00,000; Rs. 4,50,000\nDepreciation: Rs. 1,50,000 in both years\nOther expenses: Rs. 3,00,000; Rs. 2,70,000\nTax rate: 30% in 2022-23; 35% in 2023-24","image":"","options":["3.83% increase","3.69% increase","14.21% increase","11.82% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Total revenue: Rs. 31,00,000 in 2022-23 and Rs. 37,00,000 in 2023-24.\nExpenses 2022-23 = 12,00,000 − 50,000 + 4,00,000 + 1,50,000 + 3,00,000 = Rs. 20,00,000. The negative inventory change reduces expenses. PBT = 11,00,000; PAT = 70% of it = Rs. 7,70,000.\nExpenses 2023-24 = Rs. 24,70,000. PBT = 12,30,000; tax at 35% = 4,30,500; PAT = Rs. 7,99,500.\nPercentage change = $\\frac{29,500}{7,70,000} \\times 100 = 3.83\\%$.\nOption (3) treats (50,000) as an expense, (4) is the PBT change, and (2) uses the wrong base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":35,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Sona Ltd. takes 2018-19 as its base year for trend analysis. Revenue from operations was Rs. 4,00,000 in 2018-19 and Rs. 5,20,000 in 2020-21. What is the trend percentage of revenue from operations for 2020-21?","image":"","options":["30%","76.92%","120%","130%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: base year (2018-19) revenue = Rs. 4,00,000; current year (2020-21) revenue = Rs. 5,20,000.\nFormula: Trend % = (Current year figure ÷ Base year figure) × 100.\nSubstituting: (5,20,000 ÷ 4,00,000) × 100 = 130%.\nSo the trend percentage = 130%. The figure of 30% is only the increase over the base year, not the trend percentage.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":36,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"According to NCERT, analysis of financial statements provides a basis for which of the following governmental actions relating to the corporate sector?","image":"","options":["Appointment of statutory auditors","Dividend freeze","Election of the board of directors","Registration of trademarks"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that financial analysis provides the basis for several governmental actions. These are licensing, controls, fixing of prices, ceiling on profits, dividend freeze, and tax subsidy and other concessions.\nOnly dividend freeze appears in this list.\nAuditors are appointed and directors are elected by shareholders under company law. Trademark registration is unrelated to analysing financial statements.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":37,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Trend analysis may point to basic changes in the nature of a business.\nReason (R): Trend analysis takes a long-run view because it studies operational results and financial position over a series of years.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that trend analysis, with its long-run view, may point to basic changes in the nature of the business.\nR is true: trend analysis studies operational results and financial position over a series of years.\nR explains A. Because the study covers many years, it can reveal lasting shifts that a one- or two-year comparison would miss.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":38,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (User of financial analysis) with List-II (Main focus of analysis):\nList-I\n(a) Trade payables\n(b) Long-term lenders\n(c) Investors (shareholders)\n(d) Labour unions\nList-II\n(i) Present and future profitability of the firm\n(ii) Whether the firm can afford a wage increase\n(iii) Liquidity position over a very short period\n(iv) Long-term solvency and survival of the firm","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Trade payables want their claims met within a very short period, so they study liquidity: (iii). \n(b) Long-term lenders are concerned with long-term solvency and survival: (iv). \n(c) Investors concentrate on present and future profitability: (i). \n(d) Labour unions check whether the firm can afford a wage increase: (ii). \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":39,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Tool of analysis) with List-II (Description):\nList-I\n(A) Comparative statements\n(B) Common size statements\n(C) Trend analysis\n(D) Ratio analysis\nList-II\n(i) Each item of different years is expressed as a percentage of the same item in a base year\n(ii) Relationships between items of the balance sheet and the statement of profit and loss are used to assess profitability, solvency and efficiency\n(iii) Figures of two periods are shown side by side with absolute and percentage changes\n(iv) Each item is expressed as a percentage of a common item such as revenue from operations\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Comparative statements place two periods side by side and show the increase or decrease in rupees and in percent. This is horizontal analysis.\nB-(iv): Common size statements express each item as a percentage of a common base. This is vertical analysis.\nC-(i): The trend percentage relates each year's item to the same item in the base year.\nD-(ii): Accounting ratios relate items of the two statements to measure profitability, solvency and efficiency.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":40,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"A company bought its factory land in 2005 for Rs. 25,00,000. It still shows the land at this figure in its 2024 balance sheet, even though land prices in the area have risen sharply. Which limitation of financial analysis does this situation show most directly?","image":"","options":["Financial analysis does not consider price level changes","Financial analysis is affected by window dressing","Financial analysis ignores non-monetary aspects","Financial analysis is misleading when accounting procedures change"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: Financial statements record assets at their original (historical) cost. Changes in prices over time are not adjusted.\nWhy (a) fits: The land is still shown at its 2005 cost, so the analysis ignores the rise in the price level.\nWhy (b) fails: Window dressing means deliberately making the statements look better than they are. Here the figure is understated because of old cost, not manipulated.\nWhy (c) and (d) fail: The value of land is a monetary item, and no accounting procedure has been changed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":41,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Two firms in the same industry report the following for 2016-17:\nFirm X: Revenue from operations Rs. 50,00,000; Cost of goods sold Rs. 30,00,000\nFirm Y: Revenue from operations Rs. 20,00,000; Cost of goods sold Rs. 13,00,000\nOn the basis of a common size statement of profit and loss, which conclusion is valid?","image":"","options":["Firm X is better simply because its absolute gross profit is larger than that of Firm Y","Firm X earns gross profit of 40% of revenue against 35% for Firm Y, so X's cost of goods sold takes a smaller share of revenue","Firm Y earns a higher gross profit percentage because its cost of goods sold is lower in rupees","The two firms cannot be compared because they differ in size"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Common size working (revenue from operations = 100):\nX: COGS = 30 ÷ 50 = 60%, so gross profit = 40%.\nY: COGS = 13 ÷ 20 = 65%, so gross profit = 35%.\nOption (1) relies on absolute rupees, which ignores the difference in size.\nOption (3) is wrong because a lower rupee COGS does not mean a lower share of revenue.\nOption (4) is wrong because common size statements exist precisely to allow comparison between firms of different sizes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":42,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["A common size statement is also called a component percentage statement.","In a common size balance sheet, each asset is shown as a percentage of total assets.","In a comparative statement, percentage change is found by dividing the absolute change by the second year's figure and multiplying by 100.","Common size statements help compare two companies of different sizes in the same industry."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT gives 'component percentage statement' as another name for the common size statement.\n(2) Correct: total assets act as the base of 100 in a common size balance sheet.\n(3) Incorrect: the absolute change is divided by the first year's figure, not the second year's.\n(4) Correct: bringing items to a common base allows inter-firm comparison of firms of different sizes.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":43,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The Equity and Liabilities side of Sagar Ltd. shows:\nShare capital: Rs. 12,00,000 (2023), Rs. 15,00,000 (2024)\nReserves and surplus: Rs. 3,00,000 (2023), Rs. 2,40,000 (2024)\nLong-term borrowings: Rs. 4,00,000 (2023), Rs. 5,60,000 (2024)\nTrade payables: Rs. 1,00,000 (2023), Rs. 1,00,000 (2024)\nWhat is the percentage change in the balance sheet total in the comparative balance sheet?","image":"","options":["20% increase","16.67% increase","25% increase","83.33% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the four items for each year.\nTotal for 2023 = 12,00,000 + 3,00,000 + 4,00,000 + 1,00,000 = Rs. 20,00,000\nTotal for 2024 = 15,00,000 + 2,40,000 + 5,60,000 + 1,00,000 = Rs. 24,00,000\nAbsolute change = Rs. 4,00,000; % change = $\\frac{4,00,000}{20,00,000} \\times 100$ = 20%\nSo the total rises by 20%. The 16.67% option divides by the 2024 total instead of the 2023 total.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":44,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Common size statements can be used to compare the operating and financing characteristics of two companies of very different sizes in the same industry.\nReason (R): In common size statements, all items are brought to a common base by expressing them as percentages of a common total.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that common size analysis is very useful for comparing enterprises that differ substantially in size.\nR is true. Each item is stated as a percentage of a common base, such as revenue from operations or total assets.\nR explains A. Converting rupee amounts into percentages removes the effect of absolute size, so a small firm and a large firm can be compared on structure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":45,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are listed in NCERT as commonly used tools (techniques) of analysis of financial statements?\nComparative statements, Trial balance, Common size statements, Trend analysis, Ratio analysis, Bank reconciliation statement, Cash flow analysis","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT lists five tools of financial analysis: comparative statements, common size statements, trend analysis, ratio analysis and cash flow analysis. \nThe trial balance and the bank reconciliation statement are ordinary accounting procedures, not tools of financial analysis. \nSo 5 of the 7 items are tools. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":46,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the context of financial statement analysis, which of the following pairs correctly describes the terms 'analysis' and 'interpretation'?","image":"","options":["Analysis: explaining the meaning and significance of data; Interpretation: simplifying data by methodical classification","Analysis: simplifying financial data by methodical classification; Interpretation: explaining the meaning and significance of the data","Analysis: recording transactions in the books; Interpretation: preparing the balance sheet","Analysis: auditing the financial statements; Interpretation: publishing the annual report"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT says 'financial analysis' includes both analysis and interpretation.\nAnalysis means simplifying financial data by methodically classifying the information given in the financial statements.\nInterpretation means explaining the meaning and significance of that simplified data.\nOption (1) is tempting, but it swaps the two meanings.\nOptions (3) and (4) describe recording, reporting and auditing, which are not analysis tasks.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":47,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the common size statement of profit and loss of Kiran Ltd., revenue from operations of ₹20,00,000 is taken as 100. Cost of goods sold is ₹12,00,000 and operating expenses are ₹3,00,000. What percentage will be shown against Operating Income?","image":"","options":["40%","60%","25%","15%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: revenue ₹20,00,000 (base = 100), COGS ₹12,00,000, operating expenses ₹3,00,000.\nGross profit = 20,00,000 − 12,00,000 = ₹8,00,000.\nOperating income = 8,00,000 − 3,00,000 = ₹5,00,000.\nPercentage = 5,00,000 ÷ 20,00,000 × 100 = 25%.\n40% is the gross profit percentage, not operating income.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":48,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Profit before tax of Sunrise Ltd. was Rs. 8,00,000 in 2015-16 and Rs. 10,00,000 in 2016-17. The tax rate was 30% in 2015-16 and 40% in 2016-17. In the comparative statement of profit and loss, what will be the percentage change in profit after tax?","image":"","options":["7.14% increase","25% increase","6.67% increase","10% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: PBT is Rs. 8,00,000 (tax 30%) and Rs. 10,00,000 (tax 40%).\nPAT: 2015-16 = 8,00,000 − 2,40,000 = Rs. 5,60,000; 2016-17 = 10,00,000 − 4,00,000 = Rs. 6,00,000.\nAbsolute change = Rs. 40,000.\nFormula: % change = Absolute change ÷ First year figure × 100.\nSubstituting: 40,000 ÷ 5,60,000 × 100 = 7.14%.\nThe 25% option is the change in PBT, which ignores the higher tax rate. The 6.67% option wrongly divides by the second year's figure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":49,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Users of financial analysis) with List-II (Main focus of their analysis):\nList-I\n(A) Trade payables\n(B) Lenders\n(C) Investors\n(D) Economists and researchers\nList-II\n(i) Present and future profitability of the firm\n(ii) Present business and economic conditions\n(iii) Ability to pay interest and repay principal\n(iv) Ability to meet claims over a very short period\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Trade payables match (iv): they focus on short-term liquidity, that is, meeting claims over a very short period.\n(B) Lenders match (iii): they focus on the firm's ability to pay interest and repay principal.\n(C) Investors match (i): they focus on the firm's present and future profitability.\n(D) Economists and researchers match (ii): they study present business and economic conditions.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":50,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The comparative balance sheet of Ruchi Ltd. shows that its total assets were Rs. 40,00,000 on March 31, 2023 and increased by 15% by March 31, 2024. On March 31, 2024 its current assets were: trade receivables Rs. 6,00,000, inventories Rs. 8,00,000 and cash and cash equivalents Rs. 4,00,000. Its non-current assets (fixed assets and non-current investments) were Rs. 25,00,000 on March 31, 2023. What is the percentage change in non-current assets?","image":"","options":["12% increase","6.52% increase","20% increase","10.71% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total assets in 2023 = Rs. 40,00,000, rising by 15%.\nTotal assets in 2024 = 40,00,000 × 1.15 = Rs. 46,00,000\nCurrent assets in 2024 = 6,00,000 + 8,00,000 + 4,00,000 = Rs. 18,00,000\nNon-current assets in 2024 = 46,00,000 − 18,00,000 = Rs. 28,00,000\nChange = 28,00,000 − 25,00,000 = Rs. 3,00,000; % change = $\\frac{3,00,000}{25,00,000} \\times 100$ = 12%\nThe 10.71% option wrongly uses the 2024 figure as the base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":51,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"An analyst compares the profitability of Sunrise Ltd. for the year 2024-25 with that of two other firms in the same industry for the same year. This type of comparison is known as:","image":"","options":["Time series analysis","Cross-sectional analysis","Trend analysis","Cash flow analysis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Comparing a firm with other firms for the same period is cross-sectional (inter-firm) analysis.\nComparing a firm with its own performance over a period of time is time series analysis.\nHere Sunrise Ltd. is compared with two other firms for the same year, so the comparison is cross-sectional.\nTrend analysis and time series analysis both study one firm over several years, so they do not fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":52,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Common size statements help an analyst compare the operating and financing characteristics of two companies of very different sizes in the same industry.\nReason (R): In a common size statement, every item is expressed as a percentage of a common base, such as revenue from operations or total assets.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says common size statements allow comparison of companies of different sizes in the same industry, which is inter-firm comparison. \nR is true. Each item is stated as a percentage of a common base: revenue from operations for the statement of profit and loss, and total assets or total liabilities for the balance sheet. \nBecause the figures are brought to a common base, firm size no longer matters, so R explains A. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":53,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In Year 1, the common size balance sheet of Neel Ltd. showed trade payables at 12.5% and long-term borrowings at 25% of total equity and liabilities. Trade payables were Rs 3,00,000. In Year 2, the balance sheet total rose by 25%, and long-term borrowings were shown at 20% of the new total. What was the amount of long-term borrowings in Year 2?","image":"","options":["Rs 6,00,000","Rs 4,80,000","Rs 7,50,000","Rs 5,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Year 1 total = 3,00,000 ÷ 12.5% = Rs 24,00,000.\nYear 1 long-term borrowings = 25% × 24,00,000 = Rs 6,00,000.\nYear 2 total = 24,00,000 × 1.25 = Rs 30,00,000.\nYear 2 long-term borrowings = 20% × 30,00,000 = Rs 6,00,000.\nThe share fell from 25% to 20% only because the base grew; the amount did not change.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":54,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Zenith Ltd. reported share capital of Rs. 8,00,000 on March 31, 2023 and Rs. 10,00,000 on March 31, 2024. In its comparative balance sheet, what is the percentage change in share capital?","image":"","options":["25% increase","20% increase","125% increase","80% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Share capital in the first year = Rs. 8,00,000; in the second year = Rs. 10,00,000.\nFormula: Percentage change = $\\frac{\\text{Absolute change}}{\\text{First year figure}} \\times 100$\nSubstituting: Absolute change = 10,00,000 − 8,00,000 = Rs. 2,00,000; % change = $\\frac{2,00,000}{8,00,000} \\times 100$\nSo the percentage change = 25% increase. The 20% option wrongly divides by the second-year figure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":55,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The accountant of Kiran Ltd. is preparing a comparative statement of profit and loss from the following data:\nRevenue from operations: 2022-23 Rs. 20,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 25,00,000\nEmployee benefit expenses: 2022-23 Rs. 6,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 7,50,000\nOther expenses: 2022-23 Rs. 4,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 3,00,000\nTax rate: 40% in both years\nWhat percentage change will be shown against Profit before tax?","image":"","options":["45% increase","25% increase","30% increase","50% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: revenue and expenses for both years.\nPBT 2022-23 = 20,00,000 − 6,00,000 − 4,00,000 = Rs. 10,00,000.\nPBT 2023-24 = 25,00,000 − 7,50,000 − 3,00,000 = Rs. 14,50,000.\nPercentage change = $\\frac{4,50,000}{10,00,000} \\times 100 = 45\\%$.\n25% is only the growth in revenue and employee benefit expenses. The fall in other expenses adds to the growth in profit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":56,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the significance of financial analysis are correct?\n(i) A finance manager uses the tools of analysis to review actual operations constantly and to find the causes of major deviations for corrective action.\n(ii) Top management uses financial analysis to appraise individuals' performance and to evaluate the system of internal control.\n(iii) Trade payables are mainly concerned with the firm's capital structure and its survival over a long period.\n(iv) In some large companies, shareholders' interest is limited to deciding whether to buy, sell or hold the shares.\n(v) A technique frequently used by one analyst will necessarily serve the purpose of every other analyst.\n(vi) Economists and researchers analyse financial statements to study present business and economic conditions.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is financial control by the finance manager.\n(ii) Correct: this is NCERT's role for top management.\n(iii) Incorrect: trade payables focus on short-term liquidity. Capital structure and long-term survival concern lenders.\n(iv) Correct: NCERT says this about shareholders in some large companies.\n(v) Incorrect: analysts' interests differ, so a technique suited to one need not serve another.\n(vi) Correct: economists and researchers use the analysis this way.\nCorrect statements = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":57,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In a comparative statement of profit and loss of Kiran Ltd., revenue from operations is Rs. 40,00,000 in 2015-16 and Rs. 50,00,000 in 2016-17. Expenses are Rs. 32,00,000 in 2015-16 and Rs. 36,00,000 in 2016-17. What is the percentage change in profit before tax?","image":"","options":["75%","42.86%","25%","12.5%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: profit before tax (PBT) for 2015-16 = 40,00,000 − 32,00,000 = Rs. 8,00,000. PBT for 2016-17 = 50,00,000 − 36,00,000 = Rs. 14,00,000. \nFormula: % change = (absolute change ÷ first-year figure) × 100. \nSubstituting: absolute change = Rs. 6,00,000, so % change = 6,00,000 ÷ 8,00,000 × 100. \nSo the increase is 75%. Using the second year as the base would wrongly give 42.86%. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":58,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Kiran Ltd. is Rs. 12,00,000. Cost of materials and other direct costs amount to Rs. 6,60,000, and wages paid are Rs. 60,000. Following NCERT's treatment of wages, what is the gross profit percentage in the common size statement of profit and loss?","image":"","options":["40%","45%","60%","55%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = Rs. 12,00,000; direct costs = Rs. 6,60,000; wages = Rs. 60,000.\nWages are part of cost of goods sold, so COGS = 6,60,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 7,20,000.\nGross profit = 12,00,000 − 7,20,000 = Rs. 4,80,000.\nGP % = 4,80,000 ÷ 12,00,000 × 100 = 40%\nGetting 45% means wages were wrongly left out of COGS.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":59,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"When the financial performance of a company is compared with that of other firms, the analysis is called:","image":"","options":["Time series analysis","Cross-sectional analysis","Trend analysis","Horizontal analysis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT describes two kinds of comparison.\nComparison with other firms is called cross-sectional (inter-firm) analysis.\nComparison with the firm's own performance over a period of time is called time series analysis.\nTrend analysis and horizontal analysis both study the same firm over different periods, so they are not inter-firm comparisons.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":60,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Taking 2019-20 as the base year, the trend percentage of cost of materials consumed of Ravi Ltd. for 2022-23 is 80%. Cost of materials consumed in 2019-20 was Rs. 2,50,000. What was the cost of materials consumed in 2022-23?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,12,500","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 3,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: base year figure = Rs. 2,50,000; trend % for 2022-23 = 80%.\nFormula: Current year figure = Base year figure × Trend % ÷ 100.\nSubstituting: 2,50,000 × 80 ÷ 100 = Rs. 2,00,000.\nSo the cost in 2022-23 = Rs. 2,00,000. A trend below 100 means a fall from the base year.\nRs. 3,12,500 comes from wrongly dividing by 0.8.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":61,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about financial statement analysis are correct?\n(i) Analysis and interpretation are complementary to each other.\n(ii) Interpretation without analysis is easy and commonly practised.\n(iii) Through financial analysis of various firms, an economist can judge the extent of concentration of economic power.\n(iv) One objective of analysis is to assess both the short-term and the long-term liquidity position of the firm.\n(v) Analysis assesses the profitability and efficiency of the firm as a whole only, not of its different departments.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. NCERT calls analysis and interpretation complementary.\n(ii) Incorrect. Interpretation without analysis is difficult or even impossible.\n(iii) Correct. Economists use it to judge concentration of economic power and pitfalls in financial policies.\n(iv) Correct. Judging debt-repaying ability and short- and long-term liquidity is a stated objective.\n(v) Incorrect. Profitability and operational efficiency are assessed for the firm as a whole and for its different departments.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":62,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The figures in comparative statements are truly comparable only when the same accounting principles are used in preparing the statements of both periods.\nReason (R): Comparative statements are also known as vertical analysis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that financial data are comparative only when the same accounting principles are used. Any deviation should be mentioned as a footnote.\nR is false. Comparative statements are known as horizontal analysis. Vertical analysis refers to common size statements.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":63,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the comparative statement of profit and loss of Orion Ltd., Profit before tax for 2015-16 is Rs. 15,00,000 and the tax rate is 35%. For 2016-17 the tax rate is 40%, and Profit after tax shows a 20% increase over 2015-16. What is the Profit before tax for 2016-17?","image":"","options":["Rs. 19,50,000","Rs. 18,00,000","Rs. 17,55,000","Rs. 29,25,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"PAT 2015-16 = 15,00,000 × (1 − 0.35) = Rs. 9,75,000\nPAT 2016-17 = 9,75,000 × 1.20 = Rs. 11,70,000\nPBT 2016-17 = PAT ÷ (1 − 0.40) = 11,70,000 ÷ 0.60 = Rs. 19,50,000\nRs. 18,00,000 wrongly applies the 20% rise to PBT. Rs. 29,25,000 divides by the tax rate instead of (1 − rate).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":64,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Tool of financial analysis) with List-II (Distinguishing feature):\nList-I\n(A) Trend analysis\n(B) Common size statement\n(C) Comparative statement\n(D) Cash flow analysis\nList-II\n(i) Each item is shown as a percentage of a common base of the same year; called vertical analysis\n(ii) Actual movement of cash into and out of the organisation\n(iii) Each item of a series of years is related to the same item of a base year\n(iv) Figures of two periods with absolute and percentage change; called horizontal analysis","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Trend analysis relates each item of several years to the same item of a base year.\nB-(i): A common size statement expresses each item as a percentage of a common base, such as revenue from operations or total assets. It is called vertical analysis.\nC-(iv): A comparative statement shows figures of two periods with absolute and percentage changes. It is called horizontal analysis.\nD-(ii): Cash flow analysis studies the actual inflow and outflow of cash.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":65,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"While preparing a comparative balance sheet, the percentage increase or decrease shown in the last column for any item is obtained by dividing the absolute change by the:","image":"","options":["Figure of that item in the first (previous) year","Figure of that item in the second (current) year","Total of the balance sheet of the first year","Average of that item's figures for the two years"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT gives the step for the percentage column: Absolute increase or decrease (Col. 4) ÷ First year absolute figure (Col. 2) × 100.\nSo the base is the same item's figure in the first (earlier) year.\nUsing the balance sheet total as the base is the method of a common size statement (vertical analysis), not a comparative statement.\nThe current-year figure and the two-year average are never used as the base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":66,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"From the following data of Ruchi Ltd., calculate the percentage change in Total Revenue to be shown in the comparative statement of profit and loss:\nRevenue from operations: 2022-23 Rs. 30,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 36,00,000\nOther income: 2022-23 Rs. 2,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 1,60,000","image":"","options":["17.5% increase","20% increase","15% increase","18.67% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations and other income for the two years.\nTotal revenue: 2022-23 = 30,00,000 + 2,00,000 = Rs. 32,00,000; 2023-24 = 36,00,000 + 1,60,000 = Rs. 37,60,000.\nAbsolute change = Rs. 5,60,000.\nPercentage change = $\\frac{5,60,000}{32,00,000} \\times 100 = 17.5\\%$.\n20% is the change in revenue from operations alone, which ignores the fall in other income.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":67,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Tara Ltd. has revenue from operations of Rs. 30,00,000 and profit before tax of Rs. 9,00,000. Income tax is charged at 30% of profit before tax. In the common size statement of profit and loss, what percentage will profit after tax show?","image":"","options":["9%","30%","21%","25%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = Rs. 30,00,000 (base 100); PBT = Rs. 9,00,000 (30%); tax = 30% of PBT.\nFormula: Tax = 30% × 9,00,000 = Rs. 2,70,000, which is 9% of revenue.\nSubstituting: PAT = 9,00,000 − 2,70,000 = Rs. 6,30,000; $\\frac{6,30,000}{30,00,000} \\times 100$ = 21%\nThe tax rate is applied to PBT, not to revenue. 9% is only the tax line.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":68,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Analysis through comparative statements is known as horizontal analysis.\nReason (R): A comparative statement places the figures of the same item for two or more periods side by side, showing the change in it over time.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that comparative analysis is also known as horizontal analysis.\nR is true. Each item's figures for different periods are set side by side in one row. The changes are then read across that row.\nThis across-the-row comparison of one item over time is exactly why the method is called horizontal. So R explains A.\nBy contrast, vertical (common size) analysis relates items within one year to a common base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":69,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The asset figures of Meera Ltd. as at March 31, 2023 and 2024 are given below.\nList-I (Item: 2023 → 2024)\n(A) Tangible assets: Rs. 10,00,000 → Rs. 13,00,000\n(B) Intangible assets: Rs. 4,00,000 → Rs. 3,00,000\n(C) Inventories: Rs. 2,50,000 → Rs. 3,00,000\n(D) Cash and cash equivalents: Rs. 1,60,000 → Rs. 2,00,000\nList-II (Percentage change)\n(i) 20% increase\n(ii) 25% increase\n(iii) 25% decrease\n(iv) 30% increase\nChoose the correct matching.","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each percentage = change ÷ 2023 figure × 100.\n(A) 3,00,000 ÷ 10,00,000 = 30% increase → (iv)\n(B) −1,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = 25% decrease → (iii)\n(C) 50,000 ÷ 2,50,000 = 20% increase → (i)\n(D) 40,000 ÷ 1,60,000 = 25% increase → (ii)\nNote: C would wrongly show 16.67% if divided by Rs. 3,00,000, so the base must be the 2023 figure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":70,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Aditya Ltd. has current assets of Rs 6,00,000 out of total assets of Rs 10,00,000. Anjali Ltd. has current assets of Rs 5,00,000 out of total assets of Rs 12,00,000. In common size terms, by how many percentage points does Aditya's current assets share exceed Anjali's?","image":"","options":["18.33","20.00","16.67","10.00"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Aditya CA = 6,00,000, total = 10,00,000; Anjali CA = 5,00,000, total = 12,00,000.\nFormula: Item ÷ Total assets × 100 for each firm.\nAditya: 6,00,000 ÷ 10,00,000 × 100 = 60%. Anjali: 5,00,000 ÷ 12,00,000 × 100 = 41.67%.\nDifference: 60 − 41.67 = 18.33 percentage points.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":71,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Neel Ltd. earned revenue from operations of Rs. 15,00,000 and other income of Rs. 1,80,000. In its common size statement of profit and loss, what percentage will be shown against 'Total Revenue'?","image":"","options":["100%","12%","112%","88%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations = Rs. 15,00,000 (base 100); Other income = Rs. 1,80,000.\nFormula: Other income % = $\\frac{1,80,000}{15,00,000} \\times 100$ = 12%\nSubstituting: Total revenue = 100% + 12% = 112% (Rs. 16,80,000 ÷ 15,00,000 × 100).\nThe base is revenue from operations, not total revenue. So total revenue is shown above 100%, and 100% is a wrong choice.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":72,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Suppliers of long-term debt to a company are primarily concerned with which aspect of the firm?","image":"","options":["Its ability to absorb a wage increase","Its daily cash balance only","Its long-term solvency and survival","Price regulation and taxation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Step 1: According to NCERT, long-term lenders are concerned with the firm's long-term solvency and survival.\nStep 2: For this, they examine profitability over a period of time, the firm's ability to pay interest and repay principal, and its capital structure.\nStep 3: Wage affordability concerns labour unions, and price regulation and taxation concern government agencies.\nStep 4: Option (2) is too narrow, because a daily cash balance is not a long-term concern.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":73,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The workers' union of Sunrise Textiles Ltd. studies the company's financial statements before its annual negotiations. What is the main purpose of this analysis?","image":"","options":["To decide whether to buy, sell or hold the company's shares","To assess whether the company can afford a wage increase and absorb it through higher productivity or higher prices","To fix a ceiling on the company's profits","To evaluate the company's system of internal control"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: NCERT states that labour unions analyse financial statements to assess whether the firm can presently afford a wage increase.\nStep 2: They also check whether the firm can absorb that increase through increased productivity or by raising prices.\nStep 3: Buy, sell or hold decisions belong to investors.\nStep 4: Fixing profit ceilings is a governmental action, and evaluating internal control is a task of top management.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":74,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Neel Ltd. was Rs. 5,00,000 in 2014-15, Rs. 5,50,000 in 2015-16 and Rs. 6,25,000 in 2016-17. Taking 2014-15 as the base year, what is the trend percentage for 2016-17?","image":"","options":["125%","113.64%","25%","110%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Base year (2014-15) revenue = Rs. 5,00,000; 2016-17 revenue = Rs. 6,25,000.\nFormula: Trend % = (Current year figure ÷ Base year figure) × 100.\nSubstituting: 6,25,000 ÷ 5,00,000 × 100 = 125%.\nThe option 113.64% wrongly uses 2015-16 as the base. The option 25% is only the increase over the base, not the trend percentage. The option 110% is the trend percentage for 2015-16.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":75,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (tools of financial analysis) with List-II (their description):\nList-I\n(A) Comparative statements\n(B) Common size statements\n(C) Trend analysis\n(D) Cash flow analysis\nList-II\n(i) Each item of different years is expressed as a percentage of the same item in the base year\n(ii) Also known as vertical analysis\n(iii) Also known as horizontal analysis\n(iv) Study of the actual movement of cash into and out of the organisation","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Comparative statements set the figures of two or more periods side by side. This is horizontal analysis, so A-(iii).\n(B) Common size statements express each item as a percentage of a common base. This is vertical analysis, so B-(ii).\n(C) Trend analysis relates each year's item to the same item in the base year, so C-(i).\n(D) Cash flow analysis studies the actual inflows and outflows of cash, so D-(iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":76,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT an objective of analysis of financial statements?","image":"","options":["To assess the current profitability and operational efficiency of the firm","To ascertain the relative importance of different components of the financial position","To identify the reasons for change in the profitability or financial position","To record the day-to-day business transactions in the books of accounts"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four objectives of financial analysis:\n(i) assess current profitability and operational efficiency;\n(ii) ascertain the relative importance of components;\n(iii) identify reasons for change;\n(iv) judge debt-repaying ability and liquidity.\nRecording transactions is a bookkeeping (accounting) function. It happens before financial statements are prepared, so it is not an objective of analysing them.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":77,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. is preparing a comparative statement of profit and loss in the Exhibit 4.1 format. Its records show:\nRevenue from operations: 2015-16 Rs. 40,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 50,00,000\nExpenses: 2015-16 Rs. 28,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 32,00,000\nIncome tax: 30% of profit before tax in both years\nThe accountant must fill column 5 (percentage increase/decrease) for 'Profit after tax'. What is the correct entry?","image":"","options":["25.00%","33.33%","50.00%","14.29%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Profit before tax = Revenue − Expenses, so it is Rs. 12,00,000 for 2015-16 and Rs. 18,00,000 for 2016-17.\nProfit after tax = 70% of profit before tax, so it is Rs. 8,40,000 for 2015-16 and Rs. 12,60,000 for 2016-17.\nColumn 4 (absolute change) = 12,60,000 − 8,40,000 = Rs. 4,20,000 (increase).\nFormula: % change = absolute change ÷ first-year figure × 100 = 4,20,000 ÷ 8,40,000 × 100 = 50%.\nWhy the other options fail: 33.33% wrongly divides by the second-year figure. 25% is the change in revenue. 14.29% is the change in expenses.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":78,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Rainbow Paints Ltd. provides the following (Rs.):\nRevenue from operations: 2015-16 50,00,000; 2016-17 60,00,000\nOther income: 1,00,000; 1,00,000\nCost of materials consumed: 22,00,000; 26,00,000\nChanges in inventories of finished goods: (50,000); 1,00,000\nEmployee benefit expenses: 6,00,000; 7,00,000\nDepreciation and amortisation: 1,50,000; 2,00,000\nOther expenses: 4,00,000; 5,00,000\nTax rate: 35%; 40%\nWhat percentage change in Profit after tax will its comparative statement of profit and loss show?","image":"","options":["2.56% increase","11.11% increase","2.50% increase","8.60% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Expenses 2015-16 = 22,00,000 − 50,000 + 6,00,000 + 1,50,000 + 4,00,000 = Rs. 33,00,000. The negative inventory change reduces expenses.\nPBT = 51,00,000 − 33,00,000 = Rs. 18,00,000; PAT = 18,00,000 × 0.65 = Rs. 11,70,000\nExpenses 2016-17 = Rs. 41,00,000; PBT = 61,00,000 − 41,00,000 = Rs. 20,00,000; PAT = 20,00,000 × 0.60 = Rs. 12,00,000\n% change = 30,000 ÷ 11,70,000 × 100 = 2.56%. 8.60% results from adding the (50,000) as an expense.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":79,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Aditya Ltd.: Equity share capital Rs 6,00,000; Reserves and surplus Rs 3,00,000; Current liabilities Rs 1,00,000; Fixed assets Rs 4,00,000; Current assets Rs 6,00,000.\nAnjali Ltd.: Equity share capital Rs 8,00,000; Reserves and surplus Rs 2,50,000; Current liabilities Rs 1,50,000; Fixed assets Rs 7,00,000; Current assets Rs 5,00,000.\nHow many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Shareholders' funds form 90% of total equity and liabilities of Aditya Ltd.\n(ii) Fixed assets form 58.33% of total assets of Anjali Ltd.\n(iii) Current liabilities form a larger share of total funds in Anjali Ltd. than in Aditya Ltd.\n(iv) Reserves and surplus form 25% of total equity and liabilities of Aditya Ltd.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Totals: Aditya = Rs 10,00,000; Anjali = Rs 12,00,000.\n(i) Correct: (6 + 3) lakh ÷ 10 lakh = 90%.\n(ii) Correct: 7 lakh ÷ 12 lakh = 58.33%.\n(iii) Correct: Anjali 1.5 ÷ 12 = 12.5%, against Aditya 1 ÷ 10 = 10%.\n(iv) Incorrect: 3 lakh ÷ 10 lakh = 30%, not 25%.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":80,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Ruchi Ltd. has the following figures: revenue from operations Rs. 10,00,000, other income Rs. 1,00,000, and total expenses Rs. 6,00,000. Income tax is 30% of profit before tax. What will profit after tax be in the common size statement of profit and loss, as a percentage of revenue from operations?","image":"","options":["35%","28%","20%","50%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total revenue = 10,00,000 + 1,00,000 = Rs. 11,00,000 (110%).\nProfit before tax = 11,00,000 − 6,00,000 = Rs. 5,00,000, which is 50%.\nTax = 30% × 5,00,000 = Rs. 1,50,000, which is 15%.\nProfit after tax = Rs. 3,50,000, which is 35%.\nGetting 28% means other income was ignored. Getting 20% means 30 percentage points were subtracted instead of 30% of PBT.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":81,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the common size balance sheet of XRI Ltd. as at March 31, 2016, trade payables of Rs 15,50,000 are shown as 37.35%. Against which figure has this percentage been worked out?","image":"","options":["Total equity and liabilities of Rs 41,50,000","Share capital of Rs 15,00,000","Revenue from operations for the year","Total current liabilities of the company"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a common size balance sheet, total assets or total equity and liabilities is taken as the common base (100).\nCheck: 15,50,000 ÷ 41,50,000 × 100 = 37.35%, so the base is the balance sheet total.\nRevenue from operations is the base only for a common size statement of profit and loss.\nShare capital and current liabilities are individual items, not the common base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":82,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In trend analysis, the 'trend percentage' of an item for a particular year is best described as:","image":"","options":["The percentage of that item to the total assets of the same year","The percentage relationship that the item in that year bears to the same item in the base year","The percentage change in that item over the immediately preceding year only","The percentage of that item to revenue from operations of the same year"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT defines the trend percentage as the percentage relationship that each item of different years bears to the same item in the base year.\nOption (2) states exactly this.\nOptions (1) and (4) describe common size (vertical) analysis, where each item is expressed as a percentage of a common total for the same year.\nOption (3) describes a year-on-year change, which belongs to comparative statements. Trend analysis always compares with a fixed base year, not the previous year.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":83,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Revenue from operations of Neel Ltd. is Rs. 5,00,000. Other figures are: other income Rs. 50,000, cost of goods sold Rs. 3,00,000 and operating expenses Rs. 40,000. Match each item in List-I with its common size percentage in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Other income\n(B) Cost of goods sold\n(C) Gross profit\n(D) Operating expenses\nList-II\n(i) 8%\n(ii) 40%\n(iii) 60%\n(iv) 10%","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Base: revenue from operations Rs. 5,00,000 = 100.\nA: 50,000 ÷ 5,00,000 × 100 = 10% → (iv)\nB: 3,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000 × 100 = 60% → (iii)\nC: Gross profit = 5,00,000 − 3,00,000 = 2,00,000, which is 40% → (ii)\nD: 40,000 ÷ 5,00,000 × 100 = 8% → (i)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":84,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In a common size statement, each item is expressed as a percentage of an aggregate figure of which it is a part. By what other name is a common size statement known?","image":"","options":["Comparative statement","Component percentage statement","Trend percentage statement","Cash flow statement"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A common size statement shows every item as a percentage of a common aggregate, such as revenue from operations or total assets.\nEach item therefore appears as a component of that total, so NCERT also calls it a component percentage statement.\nA comparative statement is a different tool. It shows absolute and percentage changes between two periods (horizontal analysis).\nA trend percentage statement compares each year with a base year, so it is also a different tool.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":85,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the context of limitations of financial analysis, 'window dressing' of financial statements refers to:","image":"","options":["Preparing statements in a comparative form for two years","Expressing each item as a percentage of a common base","Presenting the financial statements so that the financial position appears better than it actually is","Revaluing all assets at current market prices"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: NCERT says the analyst must be conscious of window dressing of financial statements.\nWhy (c) fits: Window dressing means deliberately manipulating or arranging items so that the position looks better than it really is. For example, a firm may press debtors for payment just before the year-end.\nWhy the other options fail: (a) describes comparative statements and (b) describes common size statements. Option (d) is the opposite of the historical cost practice that analysis actually suffers from.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":86,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The Equity and Liabilities side of Neel Ltd. shows the following items:\nShare capital: Rs. 12,00,000 (2023) and Rs. 15,00,000 (2024)\nReserves and surplus: Rs. 3,00,000 (2023) and Rs. 2,00,000 (2024)\nLong-term borrowings: Rs. 4,00,000 (2023) and Rs. 6,00,000 (2024)\nTrade payables: Rs. 1,00,000 (2023) and Rs. 2,00,000 (2024)\nWhat percentage change will be shown against the 'Total' in the comparative balance sheet?","image":"","options":["25%","20%","33.33%","50%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total for 2023 = 12 + 3 + 4 + 1 = Rs. 20,00,000; Total for 2024 = 15 + 2 + 6 + 2 = Rs. 25,00,000\nAbsolute change = 25,00,000 − 20,00,000 = Rs. 5,00,000\n% change = 5,00,000 ÷ 20,00,000 × 100 = 25%\nThe 20% option uses 2024 as the base. The 50% option is the change in long-term borrowings alone, not in the total.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":87,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match the columns of a comparative statement (Exhibit 4.1 format) in List-I with their contents in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Column 1\n(B) Column 3\n(C) Column 4\n(D) Column 5\nList-II\n(i) Absolute increase (+) or decrease (–)\n(ii) Particulars\n(iii) Percentage increase (+) or decrease (–)\n(iv) Second year figures in rupees","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Exhibit 4.1 has five columns:\nColumn 1 shows Particulars, so A-(ii).\nColumn 2 shows the first year figures and Column 3 the second year figures, so B-(iv).\nColumn 4 shows the absolute increase or decrease (Col. 3 – Col. 2), so C-(i).\nColumn 5 shows the percentage increase or decrease, so D-(iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":88,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Extracts from the statements of profit and loss of Vardhan Ltd. are given below:\n2014-15: Revenue from operations Rs. 20,00,000; Expenses Rs. 15,00,000\n2015-16: Revenue from operations Rs. 24,00,000; Expenses Rs. 17,00,000\n2016-17: Revenue from operations Rs. 26,00,000; Expenses Rs. 19,50,000\nTaking 2014-15 as the base year, what is the trend percentage of profit for 2016-17?","image":"","options":["130%","92.86%","30%","140%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find profit for each year as revenue minus expenses.\n2014-15: Rs. 5,00,000; 2015-16: Rs. 7,00,000; 2016-17: Rs. 6,50,000.\nStep 2: Formula: Trend % = (Current year item ÷ Same item in base year) × 100.\nStep 3: Substituting: 6,50,000 ÷ 5,00,000 × 100 = 130%.\nThe figure 92.86% wrongly uses 2015-16 as the base. The figure 30% is only the increase over the base, not the trend percentage.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":89,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Sheetal Ltd. provides the following data for the year:\n- Revenue from operations: Rs. 10,00,000\n- Cost of revenue from operations: Rs. 6,40,000\n- Indirect expenses: 25% of gross profit\n- Other income: Rs. 20,000\n- Income tax: 30% of profit before tax\nWhat is profit after tax as a percentage of revenue from operations in its common size statement of profit and loss?","image":"","options":["20.3%","18.9%","29.0%","25.2%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Gross profit = 10,00,000 − 6,40,000 = Rs. 3,60,000 (36%).\nIndirect expenses = 25% × 3,60,000 = Rs. 90,000 (9%). Operating profit = Rs. 2,70,000 (27%).\nAdd other income Rs. 20,000 (2%): PBT = Rs. 2,90,000 (29%).\nTax = 30% × 2,90,000 = Rs. 87,000 (8.7%).\nPAT = Rs. 2,03,000, which is 20.3%.\nGetting 18.9% means other income was omitted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":90,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The Equity and Liabilities of Tara Ltd. on March 31, 2023 were:\nShare capital: Rs. 20,00,000\nReserves and surplus: Rs. 6,00,000\nLong-term borrowings: Rs. 10,00,000\nShort-term borrowings: Rs. 2,50,000\nShort-term provisions: Rs. 1,50,000\nDuring 2023-24, the following changes took place:\n- Share capital increased by 25%\n- Reserves and surplus decreased by 25%\n- Long-term borrowings increased by 20%\n- Short-term borrowings became Rs. 3,00,000\n- Short-term provisions remained unchanged\nWhat percentage change will the comparative balance sheet show against the 'Total' of Equity and Liabilities?","image":"","options":["15%","12.5%","20%","13.04%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Total for 2023 = 20 + 6 + 10 + 2.5 + 1.5 = Rs. 40,00,000\nFigures for 2024:\n- Share capital = 20,00,000 × 1.25 = Rs. 25,00,000\n- Reserves and surplus = 6,00,000 × 0.75 = Rs. 4,50,000\n- Long-term borrowings = 10,00,000 × 1.20 = Rs. 12,00,000\n- Short-term borrowings = Rs. 3,00,000; Short-term provisions = Rs. 1,50,000\nTotal for 2024 = Rs. 46,00,000\n% change = 6,00,000 ÷ 40,00,000 × 100 = 15%. The 13.04% option wrongly divides by the 2024 total.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":91,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of financial analysis as per NCERT?\n(i) It does not consider price level changes.\n(ii) It considers only monetary information and ignores non-monetary aspects.\n(iii) It may mislead if changes in the firm's accounting procedures are not known.\n(iv) It reflects the current position of the firm because statements are prepared at current market values.\n(v) It completely eliminates the role of personal judgement.","image":"","options":["Three","Two","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: price level changes are ignored.\n(ii) Correct: only monetary information is considered.\n(iii) Correct: analysis may mislead without knowledge of changes in accounting procedure.\n(iv) Not a limitation as stated: statements are prepared on the basis of accounting concepts, so they do NOT reflect the current position.\n(v) Not a limitation as stated: personal judgement is itself listed as a factor the analyst must be conscious of, so it is not eliminated.\nThree statements are valid limitations.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":92,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"A supplier sells raw materials to a company on 30 days' credit. When it analyses the company's financial statements, what is the supplier most likely to evaluate?","image":"","options":["The firm's long-term solvency and capital structure","The firm's liquidity position, that is, its ability to pay claims within a very short period","Whether the firm can afford a wage increase","The firm's present and future profitability, to decide whether to buy its shares"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: A supplier who sells on credit is a trade payable of the company.\nStep 2: Trade payables want their claims paid within a very short period, so their analysis focuses on the firm's liquidity position.\nStep 3: Long-term solvency and capital structure concern lenders, so option (1) does not fit.\nStep 4: Wage affordability concerns labour unions, and share-buying decisions concern investors.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":93,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Users of financial analysis) with List-II (Main focus of their analysis):\nList-I\n(A) Long-term lenders\n(B) Labour unions\n(C) Equity investors\n(D) Government agencies\nList-II\n(i) Whether the firm can afford and absorb a wage increase\n(ii) Price regulation and taxation\n(iii) Long-term solvency and ability to pay interest and repay principal\n(iv) Present and future profitability of the firm","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)–(iii): Suppliers of long-term debt study long-term solvency and the firm's ability to pay interest and repay principal.\n(B)–(i): Labour unions check whether the firm can afford a wage increase and absorb it through productivity or higher prices.\n(C)–(iv): Investors in shares concentrate on present and future profitability, that is, earnings.\n(D)–(ii): Government agencies use the analysis for price regulation, taxation and similar purposes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":94,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Data of Meera Ltd. (Rs.):\nRevenue from operations: 2022-23 50,00,000; 2023-24 60,00,000\nCost of materials consumed: 20,00,000; 22,00,000\nDepreciation and amortisation: 2,00,000; 2,50,000\nOther expenses: 3,00,000; 2,40,000\nHow many of the following entries in its comparative statement of profit and loss are correct?\n(i) Revenue from operations: +20%\n(ii) Cost of materials consumed: +10%\n(iii) Depreciation: +20%\n(iv) Other expenses: (25)%\n(v) Total expenses: +7.6%","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 10,00,000 ÷ 50,00,000 = 20%.\n(ii) Correct: 2,00,000 ÷ 20,00,000 = 10%.\n(iii) Incorrect: 50,000 ÷ 2,00,000 = 25%, not 20%.\n(iv) Incorrect: 60,000 ÷ 3,00,000 = (20)%. The figure of 25% wrongly uses the second year as the base.\n(v) Correct: total expenses rise from 25,00,000 to 26,90,000, so 1,90,000 ÷ 25,00,000 = 7.6%.\nThree entries are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":95,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The current liabilities of Arya Ltd. are given below:\nShort-term borrowings: Rs. 80,000 (2023), Rs. 60,000 (2024)\nTrade payables: Rs. 1,20,000 (2023), Rs. 1,80,000 (2024)\nShort-term provisions: Rs. 50,000 (2023), Rs. 40,000 (2024)\nAn accountant reports the percentage change in total current liabilities as the simple average of the three items' percentage changes. What is the correct percentage change in total current liabilities to be shown in the comparative balance sheet?","image":"","options":["12% increase","1.67% increase","10.71% increase","5% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The percentage change on a total must be worked out from the totals themselves, not by averaging item percentages.\nTotal for 2023 = 80,000 + 1,20,000 + 50,000 = Rs. 2,50,000\nTotal for 2024 = 60,000 + 1,80,000 + 40,000 = Rs. 2,80,000\nChange = Rs. 30,000; % change = $\\frac{30,000}{2,50,000} \\times 100$ = 12%\nThe item changes are −25%, +50% and −20%. Their simple average is 1.67%, which ignores the size of each item, so the accountant's figure is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":96,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In a common size balance sheet, the figure of inventories is expressed as a percentage of:","image":"","options":["Total current assets","Total assets","Revenue from operations","Shareholders' funds"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a common size balance sheet, each asset is shown as a percentage of total assets. Each liability is shown as a percentage of total liabilities.\nInventories is an asset, so its base is total assets.\nTotal current assets is tempting because inventories is a current asset. However, the common base for the whole balance sheet is the aggregate total, not a sub-total.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":97,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An analyst can fully rely on the percentage changes in a comparative balance sheet, even when one year's figures follow a different accounting policy, as long as the absolute figures are arithmetically correct.\nStatement II: When the accounting principles used in preparing the statements of different periods differ, the deviation should be mentioned as a footnote.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false: Arithmetically correct figures can still mislead. Changes in accounting procedure make the figures of the two periods non-comparable, so the analyst cannot fully rely on the percentage changes.\nStatement II is true: NCERT states that data are comparative only when the same principles are used. Otherwise the deviation must be disclosed as a footnote, so users understand that the change in figures is partly due to the policy change.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":98,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) A cash flow statement summarises the causes of changes in the cash position of a business between the dates of two balance sheets.\n(ii) A balance sheet provides information about financial position over a period of time.\n(iii) In a common size balance sheet, each liability is shown as a percentage of total liabilities.\n(iv) In a comparative statement, percentage change is calculated with the second-year figure as the base.\n(v) Comparing a firm with other firms is cross-sectional analysis, while comparing the firm's own performance over time is time series analysis.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is how NCERT describes the cash flow statement. \n(ii) Incorrect: a balance sheet shows financial position at a point in time. \n(iii) Correct: liabilities are expressed as a percentage of total liabilities. \n(iv) Incorrect: percentage change uses the first-year figure as the base. \n(v) Correct: this matches NCERT's meaning of cross-sectional and time series analysis. \nSo 3 statements are correct. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":99,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"While preparing a common size statement of profit and loss, which item is taken as the common base (equal to 100)?","image":"","options":["Total assets","Revenue from operations","Profit before tax","Total expenses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a common size statement of profit and loss, revenue from operations is taken as 100.\nEvery expense and every profit figure is then shown as a percentage of revenue from operations.\nTotal assets is the base for a common size balance sheet, not for the statement of profit and loss.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":100,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Common size statements make it possible to compare the operating and financing characteristics of two companies of very different sizes in the same industry.\nStatement II: In trend analysis, each year's figure is expressed as a percentage of the same item in the immediately preceding year.","image":"","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both statements are true","Both statements are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true: common size statements convert figures to a common base (100). This allows inter-firm comparison even when the firms differ substantially in size.\nStatement II is false: a trend percentage relates each year's item to the same item in a fixed base year, not to the previous year.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":101,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The balance sheet of Kiran Ltd. as at March 31, 2017 shows: Share capital Rs. 15,00,000; Reserves and surplus Rs. 5,00,000; Long-term borrowings Rs. 18,00,000; Trade payables Rs. 12,00,000; Total Rs. 50,00,000. In its common size balance sheet, what percentage do total shareholders' funds represent?","image":"","options":["40%","30%","10%","60%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Share capital = Rs. 15,00,000 and Reserves and surplus = Rs. 5,00,000. Total of the balance sheet (base = 100) = Rs. 50,00,000.\nShareholders' funds = 15,00,000 + 5,00,000 = Rs. 20,00,000\nFormula: % = (Shareholders' funds ÷ Total liabilities) × 100\nSubstituting: (20,00,000 ÷ 50,00,000) × 100 = 40%\nWhy the others are wrong: 30% is share capital alone, and 60% is borrowings plus payables.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":102,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"A wholesaler supplies goods on 60 days' credit to Kiran Ltd. When it analyses Kiran Ltd.'s financial statements, what will it be primarily interested in?","image":"","options":["The firm's liquidity, i.e., its ability to meet short-term obligations","The firm's capital structure and long-term solvency","Whether the firm can absorb a wage increase through higher productivity","The extent of concentration of economic power in the industry"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A supplier on credit is a trade payable of the firm.\nNCERT notes that trade payables want to know whether the firm can meet their claims over a very short period. So they evaluate the firm's liquidity position.\nCapital structure and long-term solvency are the concerns of long-term lenders, so the second option is the most tempting wrong choice.\nWage affordability is studied by labour unions, and concentration of economic power is studied by economists.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":103,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In a comparative statement, the percentage increase or decrease in an item is calculated by dividing the absolute change by which figure?","image":"","options":["The second (current) year figure of that item","The first (previous) year figure of that item","The average of the two years' figures of that item","The total of all items for the second year"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT Step 3 gives: Percentage change = (Absolute increase or decrease ÷ First year absolute figure) × 100.\nThe base is therefore the first (previous) year figure of the same item.\nDividing by the current year figure is the most tempting error. It understates an increase and overstates a decrease.\nExpressing items as a percentage of a total belongs to common size statements, not comparative statements.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":104,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Reserves and surplus of Meera Ltd. were Rs. 5,00,000 on March 31, 2023 and Rs. 4,00,000 on March 31, 2024. In the comparative balance sheet, the percentage change in reserves and surplus will be (a negative value indicates a decrease):","image":"","options":["$-20\\%$","$-25\\%$","$+20\\%$","$-16.67\\%$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: First year = Rs. 5,00,000; Second year = Rs. 4,00,000\nAbsolute change = 4,00,000 − 5,00,000 = −Rs. 1,00,000 (decrease)\n% change = −1,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000 × 100 = −20%\nSo reserves and surplus fell by 20%, shown as (20) in the percentage column. The −25% option wrongly uses the second-year figure as the base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":105,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Profit before tax of Vega Ltd. rose from Rs. 10,00,000 in 2015-16 to Rs. 12,00,000 in 2016-17. The tax rate rose from 35% to 40%. What is the percentage increase in Profit after tax?","image":"","options":["10.77%","20.00%","37.14%","9.72%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"PAT 2015-16 = 10,00,000 × (1 − 0.35) = Rs. 6,50,000\nPAT 2016-17 = 12,00,000 × (1 − 0.40) = Rs. 7,20,000\nAbsolute increase = Rs. 70,000\n% increase = 70,000 ÷ 6,50,000 × 100 = 10.77%\n20% is the rise in PBT. 37.14% is the rise in tax (3,50,000 to 4,80,000). 9.72% uses the wrong base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":106,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Read the case and answer:\nRiya, a financial analyst, is studying Kavya Ltd. She first compares Kavya Ltd.'s profit figures for the years 2020-21 to 2024-25 (Step P). She then compares its 2024-25 figures with those of its competitor Nayan Ltd. for 2024-25 (Step Q). She does this to find out why Kavya Ltd.'s net profit fell in 2024-25 even though its revenue rose (Purpose R). She describes her conclusions as 'the best possible estimates about the future', not exact certainties.\nWhich option correctly identifies P, Q and R?","image":"","options":["P: Time series analysis; Q: Cross-sectional analysis; R: Identifying the reasons for change in profitability","P: Cross-sectional analysis; Q: Time series analysis; R: Identifying the reasons for change in profitability","P: Time series analysis; Q: Cross-sectional analysis; R: Ascertaining the relative importance of components of financial position","P: Cross-sectional analysis; Q: Time series analysis; R: Judging the ability of the firm to repay its debt"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P: Riya compares the same firm over five years, which is time series analysis.\nQ: She compares the firm with another firm for the same year, which is cross-sectional (inter-firm) analysis.\nR: She wants to know why profit fell despite higher revenue. This is the objective of identifying reasons for change in profitability, not judging the relative importance of components.\nHer 'best possible estimates' wording matches NCERT's view that financial analysis is a judgemental process.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":107,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Trade payables mainly analyse financial statements to judge the firm's liquidity, that is, its ability to meet their claims over a very short period.\nStatement II: Labour unions analyse financial statements mainly for price regulation and taxation purposes.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Trade payables want to be paid within a short time, so they evaluate the firm's liquidity position.\nStatement II is incorrect. Price regulation and taxation are purposes of government agencies. Labour unions analyse statements to see whether the firm can afford a wage increase, and whether it can absorb that increase through higher productivity or higher prices.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":108,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A tool of financial analysis that a lender finds very useful may be of little use to a labour union.\nReason (R): The nature of financial analysis differs according to the purpose and interest of the analyst.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: A is true. NCERT notes that a technique frequently used by one analyst need not serve the purpose of other analysts.\nStep 2: R is true. The nature of analysis differs with the purpose of the analyst, because different users have different interests.\nStep 3: For example, a lender looks at solvency, while a union looks at wage affordability.\nStep 4: This difference in interests is exactly why a tool useful to one user may not suit another, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":109,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera Ltd. prepared trend percentages with 2019-20 as the base year.\nRevenue from operations: 2019-20 Rs. 10,00,000; 2020-21 Rs. 12,00,000; 2021-22 Rs. 15,00,000.\nNet profit: 2019-20 Rs. 2,00,000; 2020-21 Rs. 2,20,000; 2021-22 Rs. 2,40,000.\nWhich interpretation of the trend for 2021-22 is correct?","image":"","options":["Profit grew faster than revenue, so the profit margin improved","Expenses grew faster than revenue, so the profit margin fell even though absolute profit rose","Both trend percentages exceed 100, so performance is uniformly sound and needs no review","The revenue trend shows that revenue increased by 150% over 2019-20"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Revenue trend for 2021-22 = 15,00,000 ÷ 10,00,000 × 100 = 150. Profit trend = 2,40,000 ÷ 2,00,000 × 100 = 120.\nStep 2: Expenses were Rs. 8,00,000 in the base year and Rs. 12,60,000 in 2021-22. Their trend = 157.5, which is above the revenue trend of 150.\nStep 3: Profit margin fell from 20% to 16%.\nOption (1) fails because profit grew more slowly than revenue. Option (3) ignores that a slower profit trend signals a problem. Option (4) is wrong because a trend of 150 means a 50% increase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":110,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a common size statement of profit and loss, the total revenue of a company that has other income is shown at more than 100%.\nReason (R): Other income is expressed as a percentage of revenue from operations and is added to the 100% base.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. For example, if other income is 12.5% of revenue from operations, total revenue appears as 112.5%.\nR is true. Revenue from operations is the base (100), so other income is an extra percentage added above it.\nR directly explains why the total crosses 100%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":111,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Users of financial analysis) with List-II (Main purpose of analysis):\nList-I\nA. Long-term lenders\nB. Investors\nC. Government agencies\nD. Top management\nList-II\n(i) Price regulation and taxation\n(ii) Evaluating the system of internal control\n(iii) Ability to pay interest, repay principal and capital structure relationships\n(iv) Present and future profitability of the firm\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A-(iii): Long-term lenders study the firm's ability to pay interest and repay principal, and its capital structure relationships.\nB-(iv): Investors concentrate on the firm's present and future profitability.\nC-(i): Government agencies need the analysis for price regulation, taxation and similar purposes.\nD-(ii): Top management uses it to appraise individuals' performance and to evaluate internal control.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":112,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"A comparative statement of profit and loss shows that revenue from operations rose from Rs. 50,00,000 in 2022-23 to Rs. 60,00,000 in 2023-24, a 20% increase. During the same period the general price level rose by 20%. What is the percentage increase in revenue in real terms, measured at 2022-23 prices?","image":"","options":["20%","0%","40%","10%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue rose from Rs. 50,00,000 to Rs. 60,00,000, and prices rose by 20%.\nFormula: Real revenue = Current revenue ÷ (1 + rate of price rise).\nSubstituting: Real revenue = 60,00,000 ÷ 1.20 = Rs. 50,00,000.\nReal increase = (50,00,000 − 50,00,000) ÷ 50,00,000 × 100 = 0%.\nSo the 20% growth shown in the comparative statement is due entirely to inflation. This illustrates that financial analysis ignores price level changes.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":113,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"For 2016-17, Tara Ltd. reports: Revenue from operations Rs. 20,00,000; Other income Rs. 2,00,000; Total expenses Rs. 12,00,000; Income tax 25% of profit before tax. In its common size statement of profit and loss, what percentage will be shown against profit after tax?","image":"","options":["37.5%","34.09%","50%","41.67%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations Rs. 20,00,000 (base = 100); Other income Rs. 2,00,000; Expenses Rs. 12,00,000; tax 25%.\nProfit before tax = 22,00,000 − 12,00,000 = Rs. 10,00,000.\nProfit after tax = 10,00,000 − 2,50,000 = Rs. 7,50,000.\nCommon size %: 7,50,000 ÷ 20,00,000 × 100 = 37.5%.\nThe option 34.09% wrongly uses total revenue (Rs. 22,00,000) as the base. The base for this statement is revenue from operations.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":114,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a comparative balance sheet, if reserves and surplus fall from one year to the next, the absolute change and the percentage change are shown as negative figures (in brackets).\nStatement II: In a comparative balance sheet, the percentage change of an item is calculated on the figure of the current (second) year.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A decrease is marked (−) or shown in brackets; for example, NCERT shows reserves and surplus falling from 4 to 3 lakh as (01) and (25)%.\nStatement II is incorrect. The percentage change is calculated on the first-year (earlier) figure, not on the current-year figure.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":115,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (column/item of a comparative statement of profit and loss) with List-II (how it is obtained):\nList-I\nA. Absolute increase/decrease\nB. Percentage increase/decrease\nC. Total revenue\nD. Profit after tax\nList-II\nP. Second year figure minus first year figure\nQ. Absolute change ÷ first year figure × 100\nR. Revenue from operations + Other income\nS. Profit before tax − Tax","image":"","options":["A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-S","A-Q, B-P, C-R, D-S","A-P, B-Q, C-S, D-R","A-R, B-Q, C-P, D-S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–P: The absolute change (column 4) is the second year figure minus the first year figure.\nB–Q: The percentage change (column 5) is the absolute change divided by the first year figure, multiplied by 100.\nC–R: Total revenue is revenue from operations plus other income.\nD–S: Profit after tax is profit before tax minus tax.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":116,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Figures in comparative financial statements are truly comparable only when the same accounting principles are used to prepare the statements of both periods.\nReason (R): A comparative statement provides columns for the current year, the previous year, and the change in absolute as well as relative terms.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the financial data is comparative only when the same accounting principles are used. Any deviation should be shown as a footnote. \nR is true. It correctly describes the columns of a comparative statement. \nHowever, R only describes the format of the statement. It does not explain why consistent accounting principles are needed for comparability. \nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":117,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements:\nStatement I: Comparative statements are also known as horizontal analysis.\nStatement II: Common size statements are also known as vertical analysis.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. Comparative statements show figures of two or more periods side by side, and NCERT calls this horizontal analysis. \nStatement II: Correct. Common size statements express each item as a percentage of a common item within the same statement, and NCERT calls this vertical analysis. \nBoth statements are true. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":118,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Sona Ltd. has revenue from operations of Rs. 20,00,000 and employee benefit expenses of Rs. 5,00,000 for 2016-17. In its common size statement of profit and loss, what percentage will be shown against employee benefit expenses?","image":"","options":["25%","20%","33.33%","75%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations (base = 100) = Rs. 20,00,000. Employee benefit expenses = Rs. 5,00,000.\nFormula: Percentage = (Item ÷ Revenue from operations) × 100\nSubstituting: (5,00,000 ÷ 20,00,000) × 100\nSo the percentage = 25%\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":119,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A comparative balance sheet is a tool of horizontal analysis.\nReason (R): A comparative balance sheet places the figures of the same item for two or more dates side by side, showing absolute and percentage changes between them.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that comparative analysis is also known as horizontal analysis.\nR is true: a comparative balance sheet shows each item for two dates in adjacent columns, along with the absolute and percentage change.\nR explains A: the analysis is called 'horizontal' because the same item is compared across periods along a row.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":120,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The comparative statement of profit and loss of Surya Ltd. shows Revenue from operations of Rs. 40,00,000 in 2015-16 and Rs. 50,00,000 in 2016-17. What percentage change will appear in column 5 against this item?","image":"","options":["25% increase","20% increase","10% increase","125% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: First year = Rs. 40,00,000; Second year = Rs. 50,00,000\nFormula: % change = (Absolute change ÷ First year figure) × 100\nSubstituting: Absolute change = 50,00,000 − 40,00,000 = Rs. 10,00,000; % = 10,00,000 ÷ 40,00,000 × 100\nSo the percentage change = 25% increase. The 20% option wrongly divides by the second-year figure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":121,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are limitations of financial analysis?\n(i) It does not consider price level changes.\n(ii) Non-monetary aspects are ignored.\n(iii) It may be misleading if changes in accounting procedure are not known.\n(iv) It enables comparison of firms that differ substantially in size.\n(v) It is just a study of the reports of the company.","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Limitation: price level changes are ignored.\n(ii) Limitation: only monetary information is considered.\n(iii) Limitation: changes in accounting procedure can make the analysis misleading.\n(iv) Not a limitation: this is an advantage of common size analysis.\n(v) Limitation: the analysis is only a study of the company's reports.\nSo four of the five are limitations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":122,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Taking 2018-19 as the base year, the trend percentages of revenue from operations of Kiran Ltd. are 125 for 2019-20 and 150 for 2020-21. By what percentage did revenue from operations in 2020-21 increase over 2019-20?","image":"","options":["25%","50%","20%","16.67%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: trend for 2019-20 = 125; trend for 2020-21 = 150. Both are measured from the same base year (2018-19 = 100).\nFormula: % increase over the previous year = (150 − 125) ÷ 125 × 100.\nSubstituting: 25 ÷ 125 × 100 = 20%.\nSo revenue rose by 20% over 2019-20.\nThe tempting answer 25% simply subtracts the trend figures. That gap is measured in base-year points, not as a percentage of the 2019-20 figure.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":123,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"When trend percentages are calculated, the figure shown for every item in the base year is:","image":"","options":["0","100","Equal to the absolute amount of the item","The percentage of the item to total assets"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The trend percentage for any year equals (that year's figure ÷ base year figure) × 100.\nFor the base year itself, this becomes (base figure ÷ base figure) × 100, which is 100.\nThe base year therefore acts as the reference point of 100. Later years above 100 show a rise, and years below 100 show a fall.\nOption (1) is wrong because 0 would be the change from the base year, not the trend percentage.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":124,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A comparative statement of profit and loss is a tool of horizontal analysis.\nReason (R): It shows the figures of each item for two or more periods side by side, along with the absolute and percentage changes between them.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that comparative analysis is also known as horizontal analysis.\nR is true: comparative statements have columns for both years and for the absolute and percentage changes.\nComparing the same item across periods, that is, along a row, is exactly what makes the analysis 'horizontal'. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":125,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a limitation of financial analysis as stated in NCERT?","image":"","options":["It does not consider price level changes.","It ignores non-monetary aspects and considers only monetary information.","It cannot be used to compare firms that differ substantially in size.","It may be misleading without knowledge of changes in the accounting procedures followed by the firm."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Options (1), (2) and (4) are limitations that NCERT lists directly. \nOption (3) is not a limitation. NCERT says common size analysis is of immense use in comparing enterprises that differ substantially in size, because all figures are brought to a common base. \nSo option (3) contradicts the chapter. \nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":126,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"While preparing a common size statement of profit and loss as per NCERT, which figure is taken as the common base (100)?","image":"","options":["Revenue from operations","Total revenue (revenue from operations plus other income)","Profit before tax","Total expenses"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT: in a common size statement of profit and loss, each item is shown as a percentage of revenue from operations.\nThe most tempting wrong choice is total revenue. It is not the base, which is why other income makes total revenue exceed 100% (for example, 113% in Illustration 6).\nProfit and expenses are items being measured, not the base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":127,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about common size statements:\nStatement I: In a common size statement of profit and loss, each item is expressed as a percentage of total revenue (revenue from operations plus other income).\nStatement II: In a common size balance sheet, each asset is expressed as a percentage of total assets.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. In a common size statement of profit and loss, the base (100) is revenue from operations, not total revenue. In NCERT's illustration, other income appears as 13% and total revenue as 113%.\nStatement II is correct. In a common size balance sheet, each asset is shown as a percentage of total assets, and each liability as a percentage of total liabilities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":128,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The sales of Vimal Ltd. are Rs. 16,00,000 in Year 1, which is taken as the base year. Sales rose by 25% in Year 2 over Year 1, and then by a further 10% in Year 3 over Year 2. What is the trend percentage of sales for Year 3?","image":"","options":["137.5%","135%","110%","125%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Year 1 sales = Rs. 16,00,000. Year 2 sales = 16,00,000 × 1.25 = Rs. 20,00,000. Year 3 sales = 20,00,000 × 1.10 = Rs. 22,00,000. \nFormula: trend % = (current-year figure ÷ base-year figure) × 100. \nSubstituting: 22,00,000 ÷ 16,00,000 × 100 = 137.5. \nSo the trend percentage for Year 3 is 137.5%. Adding 25% and 10% to get 135% is wrong, because each year must be compared with the base year. \nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":129,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"From the following data of Meera Ltd. for 2016-17, prepare the common size statement of profit and loss. What percentage of revenue from operations will be shown against profit after tax?\nRevenue from operations Rs. 40,00,000; Other income Rs. 4,00,000; Employee benefit expenses Rs. 12,00,000; Other expenses Rs. 8,00,000; Income tax 30% of profit before tax.","image":"","options":["42%","38.18%","60%","18%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue = Rs. 40,00,000 (base = 100). Other income = Rs. 4,00,000. Expenses = 12,00,000 + 8,00,000 = Rs. 20,00,000. Tax rate = 30%.\nTotal revenue = 44,00,000\nProfit before tax = 44,00,000 − 20,00,000 = Rs. 24,00,000 (60%)\nTax = 30% × 24,00,000 = Rs. 7,20,000 (18%)\nProfit after tax = Rs. 16,80,000\nPercentage = (16,80,000 ÷ 40,00,000) × 100 = 42%\nWhy 38.18% is wrong: it wrongly uses total revenue (Rs. 44,00,000) as the base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":130,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Profit before tax of Arun Ltd. was Rs. 10,00,000 in 2022-23 and Rs. 12,00,000 in 2023-24. The income tax rate was 30% in 2022-23 and 40% in 2023-24. In the comparative statement, what is the percentage change in profit after tax (approximately)?","image":"","options":["20.00% increase","2.86% increase","2.78% increase","60.00% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Tax:\nYear 1 = 30% of 10,00,000 = Rs. 3,00,000\nYear 2 = 40% of 12,00,000 = Rs. 4,80,000\nProfit after tax:\nYear 1 = Rs. 7,00,000\nYear 2 = Rs. 7,20,000\nChange = Rs. 20,000.\n% change = 20,000 ÷ 7,00,000 × 100 = 2.86%.\nThe 20% option is the PBT change, which is not the same because the tax rate rose. 60% is the change in tax. 2.78% wrongly uses Year 2 as the base.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":131,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Suppliers of long-term debt analyse a firm's profitability over a period of time instead of looking only at its current liquidity.\nReason (R): Long-term lenders are concerned with the firm's long-term solvency and survival, that is, its ability to keep paying interest and to repay the principal.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that long-term lenders analyse the firm's profitability over a period of time and its ability to generate cash.\nR is true: their concern is long-term solvency and survival, including the payment of interest and the repayment of principal.\nR explains A: because the loan runs for many years, lenders must judge profitability and solvency over the long run, not just short-term liquidity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":132,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Government agencies use the analysis of financial statements of companies mainly for:","image":"","options":["Price regulation, taxation and similar purposes","Appraising the performance of individual employees","Deciding the credit period to be allowed to customers","Testing whether the company should change its management"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: NCERT states that government agencies need financial analysis for price regulation, taxation and other similar purposes.\nStep 2: Appraising individual performance is a use by top management.\nStep 3: Judging whether a change in management is needed is a use by investors.\nStep 4: Option (3) is a firm's own credit-policy decision and is not a governmental purpose.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":133,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran Ltd. provides the following data for 2016-17:\nRevenue from operations Rs. 25,00,000; Other income Rs. 2,50,000; Employee benefit expenses Rs. 8,00,000; Other expenses Rs. 2,00,000; Income tax 30% of profit before tax.\nWhile preparing its common size statement of profit and loss, what percentage will be shown against Profit after tax?","image":"","options":["70%","44.55%","49%","63.64%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations Rs. 25,00,000, which is the base (100%).\nTotal revenue = 25,00,000 + 2,50,000 = Rs. 27,50,000.\nProfit before tax = 27,50,000 − (8,00,000 + 2,00,000) = Rs. 17,50,000.\nTax = 30% × 17,50,000 = Rs. 5,25,000, so PAT = Rs. 12,25,000.\nPercentage = (12,25,000 ÷ 25,00,000) × 100 = 49%.\nOption 2 (44.55%) wrongly uses total revenue as the base. Option 1 (70%) is the PBT percentage.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":134,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the common size statement of profit and loss of Kavya Ltd., revenue from operations is Rs. 20,00,000 and cost of goods sold is Rs. 12,50,000. What percentage will gross profit show in the statement?","image":"","options":["37.5%","62.5%","60%","40%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations = Rs. 20,00,000 (base = 100); COGS = Rs. 12,50,000.\nFormula: Gross profit = Revenue from operations − COGS; GP % = $\\frac{GP}{Revenue\\ from\\ operations} \\times 100$\nSubstituting: GP = 20,00,000 − 12,50,000 = Rs. 7,50,000; $\\frac{7,50,000}{20,00,000} \\times 100$ = 37.5%\nSo gross profit = 37.5%. The figure 62.5% is the COGS percentage, not GP.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":135,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"While preparing a comparative statement of profit and loss, the percentage increase or decrease in an item is calculated by dividing the absolute change by:","image":"","options":["the first year's absolute figure of that item","the second year's absolute figure of that item","the average of the two years' figures of that item","the first year's revenue from operations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 3 of preparing comparative statements: % change = Absolute increase or decrease (Col. 4) ÷ First year absolute figure (Col. 2) × 100.\nThe second year's figure is not the base. Using it is the most common error.\nRevenue from operations is the base in common size statements, not in comparative statements.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":136,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"From the following data of Tara Ltd., calculate the percentage change in Profit before tax to be shown in its comparative statement of profit and loss:\nRevenue from operations: 2015-16 Rs. 30,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 36,00,000\nOther income: 2015-16 Rs. 2,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 1,60,000\nExpenses: 2015-16 Rs. 20,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 23,00,000","image":"","options":["21.67%","20.00%","17.81%","26.00%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Total revenue: 2015-16 = 30,00,000 + 2,00,000 = Rs. 32,00,000; 2016-17 = 36,00,000 + 1,60,000 = Rs. 37,60,000\nPBT: 32,00,000 − 20,00,000 = Rs. 12,00,000; 37,60,000 − 23,00,000 = Rs. 14,60,000\nAbsolute change = Rs. 2,60,000\n% change = 2,60,000 ÷ 12,00,000 × 100 = 21.67%. The 17.81% option divides by the 2016-17 figure.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":137,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Financial statement analysis is a judgemental process whose primary objective is to determine the best possible estimates and predictions about the future conditions of an enterprise.\nStatement II: Financial statement analysis is concerned only with the past and current financial position and has no bearing on the future prospects of the firm.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT calls financial analysis a judgemental process. It estimates current and past positions with the primary objective of making the best possible estimates and predictions about future conditions.\nStatement II is incorrect. Past and current data are studied precisely so that the analyst can forecast the firm's future prospects, for example to decide on further investment.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":138,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Firm P has revenue from operations of Rs. 50,00,000 and cost of goods sold of Rs. 30,00,000. Firm Q, in the same industry, has revenue from operations of Rs. 2,00,00,000 and cost of goods sold of Rs. 1,30,00,000. An analyst prepares common size income statements for both firms for the same year. Which conclusion is correct?","image":"","options":["P's gross profit is 40% of revenue against 35% for Q, so P converts a larger share of revenue into gross profit although Q earns more gross profit in rupees","Q is more efficient because its absolute gross profit of Rs. 70,00,000 is higher than P's","The comparison is invalid because common size statements can compare only different years of the same firm","Both firms have identical gross profit percentages, since cost of goods sold is 65% of revenue for each"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Gross profit: P = Rs. 20,00,000 and Q = Rs. 70,00,000.\nCommon size % (on revenue): P = 20 ÷ 50 × 100 = 40%; Q = 70 ÷ 200 × 100 = 35%.\nThe second option fails because absolute amounts mislead when firms differ in size; the common base removes this size effect.\nThe third option fails because NCERT states common size statements are useful for inter-firm comparison as well as intra-firm comparison.\nThe fourth option fails because COGS is 60% of revenue for P, not 65%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":139,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are objectives of analysis of financial statements as stated in NCERT?\n(i) To assess the current profitability and operational efficiency of the firm as a whole and of its departments\n(ii) To ascertain the relative importance of different components of the financial position of the firm\n(iii) To record business transactions in the books of original entry\n(iv) To identify the reasons for change in the profitability or financial position of the firm\n(v) To judge the ability of the firm to repay its debt and assess its short-term and long-term liquidity position\n(vi) To guarantee that the firm will earn profits in future","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Objective: judging profitability and operational efficiency helps judge the firm's financial health.\n(ii) Objective: it is listed in NCERT.\n(iii) Not an objective: recording transactions is a book-keeping function, not analysis.\n(iv) Objective: identifying reasons for change is listed.\n(v) Objective: judging debt-repaying ability and liquidity is listed.\n(vi) Not an objective: analysis only gives best possible estimates and cannot guarantee future profits.\nSo 4 statements are objectives.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":140,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In a comparative balance sheet, long-term borrowings rising from Rs. 16,00,000 to Rs. 19,00,000 and trade payables rising from Rs. 2,00,000 to Rs. 5,00,000 show the same absolute increase but very different percentage increases.\nReason (R): The percentage change of each item is calculated on that item's own first-year figure.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: both items increase by Rs. 3,00,000. Long-term borrowings rise by 3 ÷ 16 × 100 = 18.75%, while trade payables rise by 3 ÷ 2 × 100 = 150%.\nR is true: in comparative statements, % change = absolute change ÷ first-year figure of the same item × 100.\nThe two items have different first-year bases, which is exactly why equal absolute changes give unequal percentages. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":141,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Profit before tax of Vansh Ltd. was Rs. 10,00,000 in 2022-23 and Rs. 12,00,000 in 2023-24. The tax rate was 35% in 2022-23 and 40% in 2023-24. What is the percentage change in Profit after tax in the comparative statement of profit and loss?","image":"","options":["10.77% increase","20% increase","37.14% increase","5% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: PBT = Rs. 10,00,000 and Rs. 12,00,000; tax rates = 35% and 40%.\nTax: 35% of 10,00,000 = Rs. 3,50,000; 40% of 12,00,000 = Rs. 4,80,000.\nPAT: 10,00,000 − 3,50,000 = Rs. 6,50,000; 12,00,000 − 4,80,000 = Rs. 7,20,000.\nPercentage change = $\\frac{70,000}{6,50,000} \\times 100 = 10.77\\%$.\n20% is the change in PBT, and 37.14% is the change in tax. The higher tax rate pulls the PAT growth below the PBT growth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":142,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Ms. Riya, a financial analyst, carries out two exercises:\nExercise I: She compares the profitability of Alpha Ltd. for 2024-25 with its own profitability for the previous four years.\nExercise II: She compares the profitability of Alpha Ltd. for 2024-25 with that of Beta Ltd., a firm in the same industry, for the same year.\nThe two exercises are respectively examples of:","image":"","options":["Cross-sectional analysis and time series analysis","Time series analysis and cross-sectional analysis","Time series analysis in both cases","Cross-sectional analysis in both cases"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Exercise I compares Alpha Ltd. with its own past performance over a period of time. This is time series analysis.\nExercise II compares Alpha Ltd. with another firm, Beta Ltd., for the same year. This is cross-sectional (inter-firm) analysis.\nOption (1) simply reverses the two terms, which is the common mistake.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":143,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"XYZ Ltd. has a highly skilled workforce, an excellent brand reputation and very cordial labour relations. None of these strengths appear in its financial statements. Which limitation of financial analysis explains this?","image":"","options":["Financial analysis is based on historical figures","Only monetary information is considered and non-monetary aspects are ignored","Financial analysis depends on personal judgement","Financial analysis does not consider price level changes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: Financial analysis uses only information that can be expressed in money.\nWhy (b) fits: Workforce skill, reputation and labour relations are qualitative factors. They cannot be recorded in rupees, so the analysis ignores them.\nWhy the other options fail: Historical figures, personal judgement and price level changes all concern how monetary items are measured. None of them explains why qualitative strengths are missing altogether.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":144,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The finance manager of Vardhan Ltd. compares the actual expenses of each quarter with the planned figures. She notices that power costs are 18% above plan, so she investigates the causes and recommends corrective steps.\nWhich use of financial analysis does this case illustrate?","image":"","options":["Deciding the firm's credit rating for outside lenders","Financial control: constantly reviewing actual operations, analysing the causes of major deviations and taking corrective action","Judging the firm's ability to absorb a wage increase","Assessing concentration of economic power in the industry"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: NCERT states that analysis techniques help the finance manager in financial control.\nStep 2: Financial control means making constant reviews of actual financial operations and analysing the causes of major deviations so that corrective action can be taken.\nStep 3: The case shows exactly this: comparing actual with plan, finding an 18% deviation, investigating it and correcting it.\nStep 4: Credit ratings are another use, but they are not what this case shows. Options (3) and (4) relate to labour unions and economists.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":145,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about how financial analysis helps the top management of a firm:\n(i) It helps in measuring the success of the company's operations.\n(ii) It helps in appraising the individual's performance.\n(iii) It helps in evaluating the system of internal control.\n(iv) Top management is interested only in the liquidity aspect of financial analysis.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["One","Two","Three","Four"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: financial analysis helps management measure the success of operations.\n(ii) Correct: it helps in appraising individual performance.\n(iii) Correct: it helps in evaluating the system of internal control.\n(iv) Incorrect: NCERT says management is interested in every aspect of financial analysis, because it must ensure efficient use of resources and a sound financial condition.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-146","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":146,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Profit before tax of Kiran Ltd. was Rs. 8,00,000 in 2015-16 and Rs. 10,00,000 in 2016-17. The tax rate was 35% in 2015-16 and 40% in 2016-17. In the comparative statement of profit and loss, the absolute increase in tax will be:","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 70,000","Rs. 80,000","Rs. 1,30,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: PBT = Rs. 8,00,000 (35%) and Rs. 10,00,000 (40%)\nTax 2015-16 = 8,00,000 × 35% = Rs. 2,80,000\nTax 2016-17 = 10,00,000 × 40% = Rs. 4,00,000\nAbsolute increase = 4,00,000 − 2,80,000 = Rs. 1,20,000. Applying one rate to the Rs. 2,00,000 rise in PBT (for example, 35% gives Rs. 70,000) ignores the change in rate.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-147","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":147,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In a common size balance sheet, what percentage will be shown against Inventories of Rs. 1,50,000 if total assets are Rs. 30,00,000?","image":"","options":["5%","20%","0.5%","50%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Inventories = Rs. 1,50,000; Total assets = Rs. 30,00,000.\nFormula: In a common size balance sheet, total assets are taken as the base (100), so the percentage = (Item ÷ Total assets) × 100.\nSubstituting: (1,50,000 ÷ 30,00,000) × 100 = 5.\nSo Inventories are shown as 5% of total assets.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-148","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":148,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya Ltd. has total equity and liabilities of Rs 40,00,000. This is made up of share capital Rs 18,00,000, reserves and surplus Rs 7,00,000, long-term borrowings Rs 10,00,000 and trade payables Rs 5,00,000. Match each item with its common size percentage.\nList-I:\n(A) Share capital\n(B) Reserves and surplus\n(C) Long-term borrowings\n(D) Trade payables\nList-II:\n(i) 12.5%\n(ii) 25%\n(iii) 45%\n(iv) 17.5%","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Base = total equity and liabilities = Rs 40,00,000.\n(A) 18,00,000 ÷ 40,00,000 × 100 = 45% → (iii).\n(B) 7,00,000 ÷ 40,00,000 × 100 = 17.5% → (iv).\n(C) 10,00,000 ÷ 40,00,000 × 100 = 25% → (ii).\n(D) 5,00,000 ÷ 40,00,000 × 100 = 12.5% → (i).\nCheck: 45 + 17.5 + 25 + 12.5 = 100.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-149","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":149,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about preparing a comparative statement are correct?\n(i) Absolute figures in rupees relating to two points of time are listed first.\n(ii) Absolute change is found by subtracting the second year figure from the first year figure.\n(iii) A decrease in an item is indicated with a minus sign or shown in brackets.\n(iv) Comparative statements are usually prepared for the balance sheet and the statement of profit and loss.\n(v) Each item is shown as a percentage of total assets or of revenue from operations.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This is Step 1 of the procedure.\n(ii) Incorrect. The first year (Col. 2) is subtracted from the second year (Col. 3).\n(iii) Correct. A decrease is marked (–), or shown in brackets in the illustrations.\n(iv) Correct. NCERT says comparative form usually applies to these two statements.\n(v) Incorrect. This describes common size statements.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-150","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":150,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Analysis of financial statements is useless without interpretation.\nReason (R): Analysis and interpretation complement each other. Analysis simplifies the data by methodical classification, and interpretation explains the meaning and significance of that data.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that analysis is useless without interpretation.\nR is true: analysis means simplifying data through methodical classification, and interpretation means explaining its meaning and significance. NCERT describes the two as complementary.\nR explains A. Simplified data has no value until its significance is explained, which is why analysis alone is useless.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-151","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":151,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Raj Co. Ltd. had long-term borrowings of Rs 6,00,000 with a balance sheet total of Rs 27,00,000 on March 31, 2016. On March 31, 2017, long-term borrowings were Rs 9,00,000 with a balance sheet total of Rs 35,00,000. By how many percentage points did the common size share of long-term borrowings increase?","image":"","options":["50.00","29.63","3.49","20.37"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: 2016: 6,00,000 out of 27,00,000; 2017: 9,00,000 out of 35,00,000.\nFormula: Common size % = Item ÷ Total × 100.\n2016: 6 ÷ 27 × 100 = 22.22%. 2017: 9 ÷ 35 × 100 = 25.71%.\nIncrease = 25.71 − 22.22 = 3.49 percentage points.\n50% is the comparative (horizontal) change in the item itself, and 29.63% is the growth of the total. Neither is the change in common size share.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-152","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":152,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the common size statement of profit and loss of Tara Ltd., revenue from operations is Rs. 20,00,000 and cost of goods sold is Rs. 13,00,000. What percentage will be shown against cost of goods sold?","image":"","options":["65%","35%","153.85%","60%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue from operations = Rs. 20,00,000 (base = 100); COGS = Rs. 13,00,000.\nFormula: Item % = (Item ÷ Revenue from operations) × 100\nSubstituting: (13,00,000 ÷ 20,00,000) × 100 = 65%\n35% is the gross profit percentage, not the COGS percentage. 153.85% comes from using the wrong base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-153","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":153,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In a comparative statement of profit and loss, Revenue from operations of a company was Rs. 40,00,000 in 2022-23 and Rs. 50,00,000 in 2023-24. What percentage change will be shown in the percentage change column?","image":"","options":["25% increase","20% increase","10% increase","125% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: First year = Rs. 40,00,000; Second year = Rs. 50,00,000.\nFormula: Percentage change = $\\frac{\\text{Absolute change}}{\\text{First year figure}} \\times 100$.\nSubstituting: Absolute change = 50,00,000 − 40,00,000 = Rs. 10,00,000; $\\frac{10,00,000}{40,00,000} \\times 100 = 25\\%$.\n20% is wrong because it uses the second year as the base. 125% is the second year expressed as a percentage of the first year, not the change.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-154","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":154,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In some large companies, the shareholders' interest in financial analysis is limited to deciding whether to buy, sell or hold the shares.\nStatement II: Investors pay no attention to the capital structure of a firm, because capital structure affects only lenders and not the firm's earnings or risk.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Step 1: Statement I is true. NCERT states that in some large companies the shareholders' interest is limited to buy, sell or hold decisions.\nStep 2: Statement II is false. Investors are interested in the firm's capital structure to ascertain its influence on the firm's earnings and risk.\nStep 3: Investors also evaluate management efficiency and decide whether a change is needed.\nStep 4: Capital structure therefore matters to both lenders and investors.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-155","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":155,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If a company changes its method of charging depreciation between two years, the change should be disclosed as a footnote in the comparative statement.\nReason (R): Figures in comparative statements are truly comparable only when the same accounting principles are used to prepare the statements for both periods.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that any deviation in the use of accounting principles should be mentioned as a footnote.\nR is true: financial data are comparative only when the same accounting principles are used for both periods.\nR explains A. The footnote is needed because a change in method makes the two years' figures not directly comparable, so readers must be warned.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-156","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":156,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Data of Meera Ltd.:\nRevenue from operations: 2015-16 Rs. 20,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 25,00,000\nEmployee benefit expenses: Rs. 10,00,000; Rs. 12,50,000\nOther expenses: Rs. 4,00,000; Rs. 3,00,000\nTax rate: 40% in both years\nHow many of the following statements about its comparative statement of profit and loss are correct?\n(i) Employee benefit expenses increased by 25%.\n(ii) Other expenses show a decrease of 25%.\n(iii) Profit before tax increased by Rs. 3,50,000.\n(iv) The percentage increase in profit after tax is lower than that in profit before tax.\n(v) The absolute increase in tax is Rs. 1,40,000.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 2,50,000 ÷ 10,00,000 = 25%.\n(ii) Correct: 1,00,000 ÷ 4,00,000 = 25% decrease.\n(iii) Correct: PBT goes from 6,00,000 to 9,50,000, an increase of Rs. 3,50,000.\n(iv) Incorrect: with the same 40% rate, PAT (3,60,000 to 5,70,000) also rises by 58.33%, the same as PBT.\n(v) Correct: tax goes from 2,40,000 to 3,80,000, an increase of Rs. 1,40,000.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-157","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":157,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the common size statement of profit and loss are correct?\n(i) It is a form of vertical analysis.\n(ii) Every expense is expressed as a percentage of total revenue, including other income.\n(iii) It helps compare companies of very different sizes in the same industry.\n(iv) It shows the percentage change in each item over the previous year.\n(v) The percentage shown against income tax is always equal to the tax rate.","image":"","options":["2","1","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: NCERT calls common size analysis vertical analysis.\n(ii) Incorrect: the base is revenue from operations, not total revenue.\n(iii) Correct: bringing figures to a common base allows inter-firm comparison.\n(iv) Incorrect: percentage change over the previous year belongs to comparative (horizontal) statements.\n(v) Incorrect: tax is a rate on PBT. In Illustration 6, a 30% tax rate appears as 21.6% of revenue.\nSo 2 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-158","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":158,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"In the comparative balance sheet of Kiran Ltd., intangible assets (goodwill) were Rs. 8,00,000 on March 31, 2023 and Rs. 10,00,000 on March 31, 2024. What percentage change will be shown against intangible assets?","image":"","options":["25% increase","20% increase","2% increase","125% increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: First year = Rs. 8,00,000; Second year = Rs. 10,00,000\nFormula: % change = (Absolute change ÷ First year figure) × 100\nSubstituting: Absolute change = 10,00,000 − 8,00,000 = Rs. 2,00,000; % change = 2,00,000 ÷ 8,00,000 × 100\nSo the percentage change = 25% increase. The 20% option wrongly divides by the second-year figure, and 125% is the second-year figure as a percentage of the first.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-159","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":159,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The workers' union of Kiran Textiles Ltd. is studying the company's statement of profit and loss and balance sheet before negotiating with management. According to NCERT, what is the union mainly trying to judge?","image":"","options":["Whether the company should change its top management","Whether the company's shares are worth buying or selling","Whether the company can afford a wage increase and absorb it through higher productivity or higher prices","Whether the company qualifies for a tax subsidy or other concessions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Labour unions analyse financial statements for one main purpose.\nThey check whether the firm can presently afford a wage increase.\nThey also check whether the firm can absorb that increase through increased productivity or by raising prices.\nJudging management efficiency and buy/sell/hold decisions are concerns of investors.\nTax subsidies and concessions relate to government agencies.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-160","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":160,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the rate of income tax is the same in both years, the percentage change in profit after tax in a comparative statement of profit and loss equals the percentage change in profit before tax.\nStatement II: If profit before tax increases while the tax rate rises from 35% to 40%, the percentage increase in profit after tax will be lower than the percentage increase in profit before tax.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I correct: PAT = PBT × (1 − t). With the same t in both years, PAT changes in the same proportion as PBT. In NCERT's Madhu Co. illustration, PBT, tax and PAT all show 50%.\nStatement II correct: the retention factor falls from 0.65 to 0.60, so PAT grows more slowly than PBT. In BCR Co., PBT rose 46.29% while PAT rose only 35.03%.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-161","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":161,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"The Balance Sheet of Kavya Ltd. as at March 31, 2017 shows total assets of Rs. 25,00,000. Trade payables are Rs. 4,00,000. In a common size balance sheet with total equity and liabilities taken as 100, what percentage will be shown against trade payables?","image":"","options":["16%","6.25%","25%","84%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Trade payables = Rs. 4,00,000. Total equity and liabilities = Total assets = Rs. 25,00,000.\nFormula: Percentage = (Item ÷ Total equity and liabilities) × 100.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{4,00,000}{25,00,000} \\times 100 = 16$.\nSo trade payables = 16% of the total.\nWhy not 6.25%: that comes from inverting the ratio (25 ÷ 4).\nWhy not 84%: that is the share of all the other items, not of trade payables.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-162","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":162,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"Sona Ltd. has revenue from operations of Rs. 25,00,000 and a gross profit of 40% of revenue. Operating expenses are Rs. 2,50,000, excluding depreciation of Rs. 50,000. Following the NCERT treatment of depreciation, what is the operating income percentage in its common size income statement?","image":"","options":["28%","30%","12%","40%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Gross profit = 40% of revenue.\nDepreciation is part of operating expenses, so operating expenses = 2,50,000 + 50,000 = Rs. 3,00,000.\nOperating expenses % = 3,00,000 ÷ 25,00,000 × 100 = 12%\nOperating income % = 40% − 12% = 28%\nGetting 30% means depreciation was ignored.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ANALYSIS-163","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Analysis of Financial Statements","qno":163,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Analysis of Financial Statements","type":"mcq","question":"From the following data of Surya Ltd., calculate the percentage change in Profit after tax to be shown in its comparative statement of profit and loss:\nRevenue from operations: 2015-16 Rs. 40,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 50,00,000\nExpenses: 2015-16 Rs. 30,00,000; 2016-17 Rs. 36,00,000\nIncome tax rate: 2015-16 30%; 2016-17 40%","image":"","options":["40%","25%","20%","16.67%"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Revenue is Rs. 40,00,000 and Rs. 50,00,000. Expenses are Rs. 30,00,000 and Rs. 36,00,000. Tax rates are 30% and 40%.\nProfit before tax (PBT): Rs. 10,00,000 in 2015-16 and Rs. 14,00,000 in 2016-17.\nProfit after tax (PAT): 10,00,000 × 0.70 = Rs. 7,00,000 and 14,00,000 × 0.60 = Rs. 8,40,000.\nFormula: % change = (absolute change ÷ first-year figure) × 100.\nSubstituting: (1,40,000 ÷ 7,00,000) × 100 = 20%.\nOption 1 (40%) is the PBT change. Option 4 (16.67%) wrongly uses the second year as the base.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Analysis of Financial Statements","difficulty":"medium"}]});
